var e=[{topicId:`ph-general-principles`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Pharmacology is the study of how drugs and the body interact. It splits into two halves: pharmacodynamics, what the drug does to the body, and pharmacokinetics, what the body does to the drug. General pharmacology is the pharmacodynamics half — receptors, agonists, antagonists and the dose-response curve.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Drug: any substance that produces a measurable biological effect on a living system.`,`Receptor: a macromolecule (usually a protein) that a drug binds to and through which the drug produces its effect.`,`Ligand: anything that binds a receptor — a hormone, a neurotransmitter or a drug.`,`Affinity: how tightly a drug binds its receptor. Every drug that acts on a receptor must have affinity.`,`Intrinsic activity (efficacy): the ability of the bound drug to actually activate the receptor and produce a response.`,`Agonist: has affinity and intrinsic activity. Antagonist: has affinity but zero intrinsic activity.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`The four receptor superfamilies`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Family`,`How it signals`,`Example receptor`,`Example drug`],rows:[[`Ligand-gated ion channel`,`Opens an ion pore directly`,`Nicotinic, GABA-A`,`Nicotine, diazepam`],[`G-protein coupled (GPCR)`,`Via G protein and second messenger`,`Muscarinic, beta-1`,`Atropine, propranolol`],[`Enzyme-linked (kinase)`,`Receptor itself is an enzyme`,`Insulin receptor`,`Insulin`],[`Nuclear (intracellular)`,`Alters gene transcription`,`Glucocorticoid receptor`,`Dexamethasone`]],caption:`Learn one named drug for each family — this is the single most repeated general-pharmacology question.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Speed of response`,text:`Ion channel = milliseconds, GPCR = seconds, kinase-linked = minutes, nuclear = hours. The deeper into the cell the receptor sits, the slower the response.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Potency versus efficacy`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Potency = how much drug you need. It is read off the x-axis of the dose-response curve as ED50. A curve further to the LEFT is more potent.`,`Efficacy (maximal effect) = how big the effect can get. It is read off the y-axis as Emax. A HIGHER plateau means greater efficacy.`,`Example: frusemide has lower potency in milligrams than bumetanide but both are high-efficacy (high-ceiling) diuretics.`,`Example: morphine has far greater efficacy than aspirin for pain, no matter what dose of aspirin you give.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Potency is almost never what matters`,text:`Students pick the more potent drug as the better drug. Clinically efficacy matters; potency only decides the size of the tablet. A question asking for the more useful diuretic in oedema wants the high-efficacy loop diuretic, not the more potent thiazide.`},{kind:`para`,text:`An adverse drug reaction (ADR) is any noxious, unintended response to a drug at normal doses. A side effect is an unwanted but pharmacologically predictable effect — the dry mouth from atropine given for bradycardia. An idiosyncratic reaction is a genetically determined abnormal response, such as haemolysis with primaquine in G6PD deficiency.`}]},{topicId:`ph-general-principles`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`At exam level you must classify drugs by what they do at the receptor, predict how each type of antagonist shifts the dose-response curve, and compute therapeutic index. These three items account for most general-pharmacology marks.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Type`,`Intrinsic activity`,`Effect on constitutive activity`,`Example`],rows:[[`Full agonist`,`1`,`Raises to maximum`,`Adrenaline, morphine`],[`Partial agonist`,`Between 0 and 1`,`Submaximal rise`,`Buprenorphine, pindolol`],[`Antagonist (neutral)`,`0`,`No change`,`Naloxone, propranolol`],[`Inverse agonist`,`Between 0 and -1`,`Lowers below baseline`,`Ranitidine at H2, some BZD beta-carbolines`]],caption:`Intrinsic activity (alpha) runs from +1 through 0 to -1. An inverse agonist needs a receptor with constitutive (agonist-free) activity.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Partial agonist in the presence of a full agonist`,text:`A partial agonist behaves as an ANTAGONIST when a full agonist is already present, because it occupies receptors but produces less than the maximal response. This is why pindolol reduces the effect of endogenous adrenaline yet still supports resting heart rate.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Competitive versus non-competitive antagonism`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Competitive`,`Non-competitive`],rows:[[`Binding site`,`Same as agonist (orthosteric)`,`Allosteric or irreversible covalent`],[`Effect on curve`,`Parallel shift to the RIGHT`,`Shift down (curve flattened)`],[`Emax`,`Unchanged`,`Reduced`],[`ED50 / apparent affinity`,`Increased`,`Unchanged or increased`],[`Surmountable by more agonist`,`Yes`,`No`],[`Example`,`Atropine vs ACh; naloxone vs morphine`,`Phenoxybenzamine vs adrenaline; ketamine at NMDA`]],caption:`The curve behaviour is the discriminator: right-shift with same peak = competitive; lower peak = non-competitive.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Physiological versus pharmacological antagonism`,text:`Adrenaline reversing histamine-induced bronchospasm is PHYSIOLOGICAL (functional) antagonism — two different receptors producing opposite effects. Chlorpheniramine blocking histamine at H1 is pharmacological antagonism. Protamine neutralising heparin is CHEMICAL antagonism.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Therapeutic Index (TI) = LD50 / ED50`,caption:`LD50 = dose lethal to 50 percent of animals; ED50 = dose producing the desired effect in 50 percent. In humans TD50 replaces LD50. Higher TI = safer drug.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Certain Safety Factor (Standard Safety Margin) = LD1 / ED99`,caption:`LD1 = dose lethal to 1 percent; ED99 = dose effective in 99 percent. A more conservative index because it uses the overlapping tails, not the midpoints.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Worked example: a drug has ED50 = 4 mg/kg and LD50 = 200 mg/kg. TI = 200 / 4 = 50. A second drug has ED50 = 2 mg/kg and LD50 = 40 mg/kg, so TI = 20. The second drug is more potent (lower ED50) but less safe (lower TI). Potency and safety are independent.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Loss of effect on repeated dosing`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Term`,`Time course`,`Mechanism`,`Example`],rows:[[`Tolerance`,`Days to weeks`,`Receptor down-regulation, enzyme induction`,`Morphine, barbiturates`],[`Tachyphylaxis`,`Minutes to hours`,`Depletion of mediator, receptor phosphorylation`,`Ephedrine, tyramine, nitrates`],[`Drug resistance`,`Variable`,`Change in the pathogen or tumour cell, not the host`,`Antibiotic resistance`],[`Idiosyncrasy`,`First dose`,`Genetic enzyme or protein variant`,`Primaquine haemolysis in G6PD deficiency`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Tachyphylaxis`,text:`Tachy = fast. Tachyphylaxis is the FAST one, classically with indirectly acting sympathomimetics such as ephedrine and tyramine, whose effect fades as the noradrenaline stores empty.`}]},{topicId:`ph-general-principles`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The high-scoring band of general pharmacology is quantitative: spare receptors, the Schild relationship, the WHO ADR classification A to F, and the enzyme-level basis of interactions.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`E / Emax = [D] / ([D] + KD)`,caption:`E = effect at concentration [D]; Emax = maximal effect; KD = dissociation constant, the concentration occupying half the receptors. When effect is directly proportional to occupancy, EC50 equals KD.`},{kind:`para`,text:`If a full response occurs while only a fraction of receptors are occupied, the surplus receptors are called spare receptors (receptor reserve). Here EC50 is much lower than KD. Spare receptors explain why an irreversible antagonist first shifts the agonist curve to the right (reserve absorbs the loss) and only then depresses Emax once the reserve is used up — a curve pattern that looks competitive at low antagonist concentrations and non-competitive at high ones.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Dose ratio - 1 = [B] / KB     and     pA2 = -log KB`,caption:`Schild equation. Dose ratio = agonist EC50 with antagonist divided by EC50 without; [B] = antagonist concentration; KB = antagonist dissociation constant. pA2 is the negative log of the antagonist concentration that makes the dose ratio 2, i.e. that requires a doubling of agonist dose.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`pA2 interpretation`,text:`A higher pA2 means a more potent competitive antagonist. A Schild plot slope of 1 confirms simple competitive antagonism; a slope other than 1 indicates non-competitive or allosteric behaviour.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Type`,`Name`,`Nature`,`Example`],rows:[[`A`,`Augmented`,`Dose-dependent, predictable, common, low mortality`,`Bleeding with warfarin; hypoglycaemia with insulin`],[`B`,`Bizarre`,`Dose-independent, unpredictable, rare, high mortality`,`Penicillin anaphylaxis; halothane hepatitis`],[`C`,`Chronic (continuous)`,`Related to cumulative dose and duration`,`HPA suppression with steroids; analgesic nephropathy`],[`D`,`Delayed`,`Appears long after exposure`,`Teratogenesis with thalidomide; carcinogenesis`],[`E`,`End of use`,`Withdrawal on stopping the drug`,`Rebound hypertension on clonidine withdrawal`],[`F`,`Failure of therapy`,`Unexpected therapeutic failure, often an interaction`,`Oral contraceptive failure with rifampicin`]],caption:`Rawlins-Thompson classification, extended to F. Types A and B carry the most marks: A is augmented pharmacology, B is bizarre and immunological or idiosyncratic.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`A to F`,text:`Augmented, Bizarre, Chronic, Delayed, End-of-use, Failure.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Mechanism`,`Perpetrator`,`Consequence`],rows:[[`CYP induction`,`Rifampicin, phenytoin, carbamazepine, phenobarbitone`,`Loss of effect of warfarin, OCPs, ciclosporin`],[`CYP inhibition`,`Ketoconazole, erythromycin, cimetidine, ritonavir`,`Toxicity of terfenadine, statins, warfarin`],[`Displacement from albumin`,`Aspirin, sulphonamides, phenylbutazone`,`Transient rise in free warfarin or tolbutamide`],[`P-glycoprotein inhibition`,`Verapamil, quinidine, ciclosporin`,`Digoxin toxicity`],[`Pharmacodynamic addition`,`Alcohol plus benzodiazepine`,`Excess CNS depression`]],caption:`Drug interactions worth memorising: the perpetrator drug and the clinical consequence.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Enzyme induction takes time`,text:`Induction requires new protein synthesis, so it develops over days to two weeks and wears off equally slowly. Inhibition is immediate — competitive occupancy of the enzyme. A question describing INR rising within 24 hours of adding a drug points to inhibition, not induction.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Two more definitions that separate careful candidates: a hit-and-run drug produces an effect long outlasting its plasma presence because it acts irreversibly (aspirin on platelet COX, omeprazole on H+/K+ ATPase, phenoxybenzamine on alpha receptors). A prodrug is inactive until metabolised (enalapril to enalaprilat, levodopa to dopamine, cyclophosphamide to phosphoramide mustard).`}]},{topicId:`ph-ans-cholinergic`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The parasympathetic nervous system uses acetylcholine (ACh) as its transmitter. Drugs that increase cholinergic activity are cholinomimetics or parasympathomimetics; drugs that block it are anticholinergics. Almost every effect in this topic follows from one phrase: rest and digest.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Life cycle of acetylcholine`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Choline is taken into the nerve terminal by a carrier that hemicholinium blocks.`,`Choline acetyltransferase (ChAT) joins choline to acetyl-CoA to make ACh.`,`ACh is packed into vesicles by a transporter that vesamicol blocks.`,`A nerve impulse lets calcium in and the vesicle releases ACh; botulinum toxin blocks this release.`,`ACh acts on muscarinic or nicotinic receptors on the target cell.`,`Acetylcholinesterase (AChE) splits ACh into choline and acetate within milliseconds; choline is recycled.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Two cholinesterases`,text:`True (acetyl) cholinesterase sits at synapses and on red cells and is specific for ACh. Pseudocholinesterase (butyrylcholinesterase) is made in the liver, circulates in plasma, and hydrolyses succinylcholine, procaine and ester local anaesthetics.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`What stimulating muscarinic receptors does`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Eye: pupil constricts (miosis) and the lens thickens for near vision (accommodation for near). Intraocular pressure falls.`,`Heart: rate slows (bradycardia) and AV conduction slows.`,`Lungs: bronchi constrict and secretions increase.`,`Gut: motility and secretion increase, sphincters relax.`,`Bladder: detrusor contracts, sphincter relaxes, so the bladder empties.`,`Glands: sweating, salivation, lacrimation all increase.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Cholinergic excess = DUMBBELSS`,text:`Diarrhoea, Urination, Miosis, Bradycardia, Bronchospasm, Emesis, Lacrimation, Salivation, Sweating. Anticholinergic excess is the exact opposite: dry as a bone, hot as a hare, red as a beet, blind as a bat, mad as a hatter.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Atropine, from Atropa belladonna, is the model antimuscarinic. It blocks muscarinic receptors, so it dilates the pupil, dries secretions, speeds the heart, relaxes gut and bladder, and in overdose causes hot dry flushed skin, fever and delirium. Its main uses are as a preanaesthetic drying agent, for organophosphate poisoning and for symptomatic bradycardia.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Nicotinic receptors sit at the skeletal neuromuscular junction (Nm) and in autonomic ganglia and the adrenal medulla (Nn). Blocking Nm gives muscle paralysis, which is exactly what surgeons want during abdominal surgery — that is the neuromuscular blocker group.`}]},{topicId:`ph-ans-cholinergic`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The exam material here is receptor subtype mapping, the direct versus indirect cholinomimetic split, the reversible versus irreversible anticholinesterase split, and the two classes of neuromuscular blocker.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Receptor`,`Coupling`,`Main location`,`Effect of activation`],rows:[[`M1`,`Gq (IP3/DAG)`,`CNS, gastric parietal cells, ganglia`,`Gastric acid secretion, cognition`],[`M2`,`Gi (cAMP down)`,`Heart (SA and AV node)`,`Bradycardia, slowed AV conduction`],[`M3`,`Gq (IP3/DAG)`,`Smooth muscle, glands, eye`,`Bronchoconstriction, secretion, miosis`],[`M4 and M5`,`Gi and Gq`,`CNS mainly`,`Modulation of dopamine release, locomotion`],[`Nm`,`Ion channel (Na/K)`,`Skeletal muscle end plate`,`Muscle contraction`],[`Nn`,`Ion channel (Na/K)`,`Autonomic ganglia, adrenal medulla`,`Ganglionic transmission, catecholamine release`]],caption:`Cholinergic receptor subtypes. Odd-numbered muscarinic receptors (M1, M3, M5) are Gq; even-numbered (M2, M4) are Gi.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`M-receptor coupling`,text:`Odd = Q (M1, M3, M5 are Gq). Even = I (M2, M4 are Gi). Pirenzepine is the M1 blocker, and pirenzepine reduces acid — because M1 is on the parietal cell pathway.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug`,`Type`,`Resistant to AChE?`,`Chief use`],rows:[[`Acetylcholine`,`Direct, choline ester`,`No`,`Miosis during cataract surgery only`],[`Bethanechol`,`Direct, muscarinic selective`,`Yes`,`Postoperative urinary retention, paralytic ileus`],[`Carbachol`,`Direct, M and N`,`Yes`,`Glaucoma (topical)`],[`Methacholine`,`Direct, mainly M`,`Partly`,`Bronchial hyperreactivity test`],[`Pilocarpine`,`Direct alkaloid, M3`,`Yes`,`Glaucoma, xerostomia after radiotherapy`],[`Neostigmine`,`Indirect, reversible`,`Not applicable`,`Myasthenia, reversal of NM block, ileus`]],caption:`Cholinomimetics: direct agents act on the receptor, indirect agents raise ACh by inhibiting cholinesterase.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Anticholinesterases`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Group`,`Bond to enzyme`,`Duration`,`Examples`,`CNS entry`],rows:[[`Simple alcohol`,`Hydrogen (reversible)`,`5-15 minutes`,`Edrophonium`,`No`],[`Carbamate`,`Carbamylated (reversible)`,`3-8 hours`,`Neostigmine, pyridostigmine, physostigmine, rivastigmine`,`Only physostigmine, donepezil, rivastigmine`],[`Organophosphate`,`Phosphorylated (irreversible)`,`Days to weeks`,`Parathion, malathion, dyflos, echothiophate`,`Yes`]],caption:`Neostigmine carries a quaternary nitrogen so it stays out of the CNS; physostigmine is a tertiary amine and enters the brain.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Neostigmine versus physostigmine`,text:`Neostigmine additionally stimulates the nicotinic end plate directly, which is why it is preferred in myasthenia gravis and for reversing non-depolarising blockade. Physostigmine is the one used for central antimuscarinic (atropine or datura) poisoning because it crosses the blood-brain barrier.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Organophosphate poisoning`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Decontaminate: remove clothing, wash skin, secure airway, give oxygen.`,`Atropine 2 mg IV repeated every 5-10 minutes until atropinisation — dry secretions, clear chest, heart rate above 80. Atropine reverses only the muscarinic features.`,`Pralidoxime (2-PAM) 1-2 g IV reactivates the phosphorylated enzyme and is the only agent that relieves the nicotinic features (fasciculations, muscle weakness, respiratory paralysis).`,`Give pralidoxime EARLY, before ageing of the enzyme makes the bond permanent.`,`Diazepam for convulsions; ventilatory support as needed.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Atropine alone is not enough`,text:`Atropine does nothing for muscle fasciculation and respiratory muscle paralysis because those are nicotinic. That is why pralidoxime must be added. Conversely pralidoxime alone does not control secretions.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Antimuscarinics and their niches`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug`,`Feature`,`Chief use`],rows:[[`Atropine`,`Tertiary amine, crosses BBB`,`OP poisoning, bradycardia, preanaesthetic`],[`Hyoscine (scopolamine)`,`Strong central sedative and antiemetic`,`Motion sickness (transdermal)`],[`Ipratropium / tiotropium`,`Inhaled quaternary, not absorbed`,`COPD, asthma`],[`Glycopyrrolate`,`Quaternary, no CNS effect`,`Preanaesthetic drying, with neostigmine reversal`],[`Dicyclomine / hyoscine butylbromide`,`Antispasmodic`,`Intestinal and renal colic`],[`Tropicamide / homatropine`,`Short-acting mydriatic`,`Fundus examination, refraction`],[`Oxybutynin / tolterodine / solifenacin`,`Bladder M3 selective`,`Overactive bladder`]]}]},{topicId:`ph-ans-cholinergic`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The advanced band covers neuromuscular blockade in detail, myasthenia gravis testing, and the chemistry of enzyme reactivation.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Neuromuscular blockers`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Depolarising (succinylcholine)`,`Non-depolarising (competitive)`],rows:[[`Action at Nm`,`Persistent agonist, sustained depolarisation`,`Competitive antagonist`],[`Initial fasciculations`,`Present`,`Absent`],[`Effect of neostigmine`,`Worsens phase I block`,`Reverses the block`],[`Fade on tetanic stimulation`,`Absent in phase I`,`Present`],[`Post-tetanic potentiation`,`Absent in phase I`,`Present`],[`Effect of ether / halothane`,`Little change`,`Block potentiated`],[`Postoperative muscle pain`,`Common`,`Absent`],[`Examples`,`Succinylcholine (suxamethonium)`,`d-Tubocurarine, pancuronium, vecuronium, atracurium, rocuronium`]],caption:`Fade and post-tetanic potentiation are the classic bedside discriminators; both are features of competitive block.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Succinylcholine hazards`,text:`It releases potassium from muscle, so it is contraindicated in burns, crush injury, spinal cord injury and hyperkalaemia — risk of cardiac arrest. It also triggers malignant hyperthermia with halothane. Prolonged apnoea occurs in atypical pseudocholinesterase (low dibucaine number).`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Atracurium is eliminated by spontaneous Hofmann degradation, so it is the agent of choice in renal and hepatic failure. Its metabolite laudanosine can cause seizures at very high dose.`,`Rocuronium has the fastest onset among non-depolarising agents and is reversed specifically by sugammadex.`,`Pancuronium blocks cardiac M2 receptors and causes tachycardia; vecuronium is cardiostable.`,`d-Tubocurarine causes histamine release and hypotension and ganglion block.`,`Mivacurium is the only non-depolarising agent hydrolysed by plasma cholinesterase.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Myasthenia gravis`},{kind:`para`,text:`Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune disease in which antibodies destroy nicotinic Nm receptors at the end plate, producing fatiguable weakness. Treatment is pyridostigmine or neostigmine, with thymectomy, corticosteroids, azathioprine and, in crisis, plasmapheresis or IV immunoglobulin.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Myasthenic crisis`,`Cholinergic crisis`],rows:[[`Cause`,`Too little anticholinesterase`,`Overdose of anticholinesterase`],[`Pupils`,`Normal or dilated`,`Constricted (miosis)`],[`Secretions and gut`,`Normal`,`Excess salivation, sweating, diarrhoea`],[`Edrophonium test`,`Weakness improves`,`Weakness worsens`],[`Management`,`Increase the dose`,`Withhold drug, give atropine, ventilate`]],caption:`The edrophonium (Tensilon) test separates the two because edrophonium acts within seconds and lasts only minutes.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Ageing of the enzyme`,text:`After an organophosphate phosphorylates AChE, one alkoxy group is lost by hydrolysis. The remaining bond becomes irreversible — this is ageing. Once ageing has occurred pralidoxime cannot reactivate the enzyme, and recovery must wait for new enzyme synthesis (weeks). Ageing is fast with soman and slow with parathion.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Pralidoxime is contraindicated in carbamate (carbaryl) poisoning — the carbamylated enzyme reactivates spontaneously and oxime may worsen the block.`,`Pralidoxime is quaternary and poorly CNS-penetrant; obidoxime and the pro-drug pro-2-PAM penetrate better.`,`Organophosphate-induced delayed polyneuropathy is caused by inhibition of neuropathy target esterase (NTE), typically 1-3 weeks later, and is not helped by atropine or oximes.`,`Central antimuscarinic syndrome from atropine or Datura is treated with physostigmine, never neostigmine.`]}]},{topicId:`ph-ans-adrenergic`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The sympathetic nervous system uses noradrenaline at most tissues and adrenaline from the adrenal medulla. Drugs that mimic it are sympathomimetics or adrenergic agonists; drugs that block it are adrenergic blockers. Every effect follows from one phrase: fight or flight.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`How catecholamines are made`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Tyrosine enters the nerve terminal.`,`Tyrosine hydroxylase converts it to DOPA. This is the rate-limiting step and is blocked by metirosine.`,`DOPA decarboxylase converts DOPA to dopamine. Carbidopa blocks this enzyme outside the brain.`,`Dopamine beta-hydroxylase inside the vesicle converts dopamine to noradrenaline.`,`In the adrenal medulla only, PNMT methylates noradrenaline to adrenaline.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Rate-limiting step`,text:`Tyrosine hydroxylase is the rate-limiting enzyme of catecholamine synthesis. This single fact appears almost every year.`},{kind:`para`,text:`The action of released noradrenaline is ended mainly by re-uptake back into the nerve terminal (uptake-1, blocked by cocaine and imipramine), not by enzymes. Only after re-uptake is it broken down by monoamine oxidase (MAO) inside the neurone, while catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT) acts mainly on circulating catecholamines outside the neurone. The final urinary metabolite is VMA (vanillylmandelic acid).`},{kind:`heading`,text:`What the adrenergic receptors do`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Alpha-1: blood vessels constrict (blood pressure rises), pupil dilates (mydriasis), bladder sphincter and prostate contract.`,`Alpha-2: sits mostly on the nerve terminal itself and switches off further noradrenaline release; in the brain it lowers sympathetic outflow and blood pressure.`,`Beta-1: heart rate, force and conduction all increase; renin is released from the kidney.`,`Beta-2: bronchi dilate, uterus relaxes, skeletal muscle vessels dilate, liver glycogen breaks down.`,`Beta-3: fat is broken down in adipose tissue and the bladder detrusor relaxes.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`One heart, two lungs`,text:`Beta-1 for the ONE heart, beta-2 for the TWO lungs. So a cardioselective blocker is a beta-1 blocker and a bronchodilator is a beta-2 agonist.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Salbutamol is the model beta-2 agonist for asthma; propranolol is the model non-selective beta blocker; prazosin is the model alpha-1 blocker for hypertension and benign prostatic hyperplasia; clonidine is the model central alpha-2 agonist.`}]},{topicId:`ph-ans-adrenergic`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Exam questions cluster on the receptor location table, the dose-dependent behaviour of dopamine and adrenaline, and the classification of beta blockers with their contraindications.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Receptor`,`G protein / effector`,`Key sites`,`Response`],rows:[[`Alpha-1`,`Gq, IP3 and DAG, Ca up`,`Vascular smooth muscle, radial iris, prostate`,`Vasoconstriction, mydriasis, urinary retention`],[`Alpha-2`,`Gi, cAMP down`,`Presynaptic terminal, CNS, platelets, pancreas`,`Less NA release, sedation, BP fall, platelet aggregation, less insulin`],[`Beta-1`,`Gs, cAMP up`,`Heart, juxtaglomerular cells`,`Tachycardia, positive inotropy, renin release`],[`Beta-2`,`Gs, cAMP up`,`Bronchi, uterus, skeletal muscle vessels, liver`,`Bronchodilation, tocolysis, vasodilation, glycogenolysis`],[`Beta-3`,`Gs, cAMP up`,`Adipose tissue, bladder detrusor`,`Lipolysis, detrusor relaxation (mirabegron)`],[`D1`,`Gs, cAMP up`,`Renal and mesenteric vessels`,`Vasodilation, natriuresis`]],caption:`Adrenergic receptor map. All beta receptors are Gs; alpha-1 is Gq and alpha-2 is Gi, the only inhibitory adrenergic receptor.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Classification of sympathomimetics`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Mechanism`,`Examples`,`Note`],rows:[[`Direct acting`,`Bind the receptor`,`Adrenaline, noradrenaline, phenylephrine, salbutamol`,`Effect unchanged after reserpine`],[`Indirect acting`,`Release stored NA / block uptake`,`Tyramine, amphetamine, cocaine`,`Effect abolished by reserpine; tachyphylaxis`],[`Mixed acting`,`Both`,`Ephedrine, dopamine, metaraminol`,`Partial loss after reserpine`]],caption:`Reserpine depletes vesicular stores, so it is the classic tool that separates direct from indirect agents.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Dopamine is dose dependent`,text:`Low dose (1-2 mcg/kg/min) acts on D1 to dilate renal vessels. Intermediate dose (2-10) adds beta-1 inotropy. High dose (above 10) recruits alpha-1 and causes vasoconstriction, losing the renal benefit. Dobutamine, by contrast, is a selective beta-1 agonist with little effect on rate.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Adrenaline reversal (Dale vinculum)`,text:`Adrenaline normally raises blood pressure through alpha-1. After an alpha blocker such as phentolamine, the unopposed beta-2 vasodilation makes the SAME dose of adrenaline LOWER the blood pressure. Noradrenaline is not reversed because it has negligible beta-2 action.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Alpha blockers`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug`,`Selectivity`,`Nature of block`,`Use`],rows:[[`Phenoxybenzamine`,`Non-selective alpha`,`Irreversible, covalent, 24-48 h`,`Preoperative phaeochromocytoma`],[`Phentolamine`,`Non-selective alpha`,`Competitive, reversible, minutes`,`Hypertensive crisis, NA extravasation`],[`Prazosin / terazosin / doxazosin`,`Alpha-1`,`Competitive`,`Hypertension, BPH`],[`Tamsulosin`,`Alpha-1A`,`Competitive`,`BPH with least postural hypotension`],[`Yohimbine`,`Alpha-2`,`Competitive`,`Investigational, raises NA release`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`First-dose phenomenon`,text:`Prazosin causes marked postural hypotension and syncope after the first dose. Start with a low bedtime dose. Ergotamine, in contrast, is a partial alpha agonist and is used in migraine, not hypertension.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Beta blockers`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Subgroup`,`Members`,`Distinguishing feature`],rows:[[`Non-selective, no ISA`,`Propranolol, timolol, nadolol, sotalol`,`Propranolol has high MSA; sotalol also class III`],[`Cardioselective (beta-1)`,`Atenolol, metoprolol, bisoprolol, esmolol`,`Safer in asthma and diabetes; esmolol lasts 9 min`],[`With ISA (partial agonist)`,`Pindolol, acebutolol, celiprolol`,`Less bradycardia and less lipid disturbance`],[`Alpha plus beta blocker`,`Labetalol, carvedilol`,`Labetalol in pregnancy-induced hypertension`],[`Vasodilator beta blocker`,`Nebivolol, celiprolol`,`Nebivolol releases nitric oxide`]],caption:`ISA = intrinsic sympathomimetic activity; MSA = membrane stabilising (local anaesthetic) activity, which contributes nothing therapeutically.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Uses: hypertension, angina, post-myocardial infarction, arrhythmias, chronic heart failure (metoprolol, bisoprolol, carvedilol), thyrotoxicosis, migraine prophylaxis, essential tremor, glaucoma (timolol), anxiety.`,`Contraindications: bronchial asthma, severe bradycardia and second or third degree heart block, decompensated heart failure, variant (Prinzmetal) angina, severe peripheral vascular disease.`,`Cautions in diabetes: beta blockade masks the tachycardia and tremor of hypoglycaemia and delays glycogenolytic recovery. Sweating is preserved.`,`Abrupt withdrawal after long use causes rebound angina, arrhythmia or infarction due to receptor up-regulation. Taper over 1-2 weeks.`]}]},{topicId:`ph-ans-adrenergic`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced adrenergic pharmacology rewards the metabolism map, the false-transmitter concept and the exact reason a given blocker is chosen in phaeochromocytoma.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Catecholamine metabolism`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`MAO-A prefers noradrenaline and serotonin and is found in gut and liver; MAO-B prefers dopamine and phenylethylamine and predominates in the brain.`,`Selegiline and rasagiline are selective MAO-B inhibitors used in Parkinson disease; clorgyline and moclobemide inhibit MAO-A.`,`COMT requires S-adenosylmethionine and magnesium; entacapone and tolcapone are its inhibitors, tolcapone being the one that crosses into the brain and carries hepatotoxicity risk.`,`Noradrenaline yields normetanephrine then VMA; dopamine yields homovanillic acid (HVA); serotonin yields 5-HIAA.`,`Uptake-1 (neuronal, NET) is the main terminator of action and is blocked by cocaine, tricyclic antidepressants and amphetamine. Uptake-2 is extraneuronal and low affinity, blocked by steroids.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Phaeochromocytoma sequence`,text:`Give the ALPHA blocker (phenoxybenzamine) FIRST, then add a beta blocker. Starting with a beta blocker leaves alpha-mediated vasoconstriction unopposed and can precipitate a hypertensive crisis. Diagnostic marker: raised urinary metanephrines and VMA.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Agent`,`Alpha-1`,`Beta-1`,`Beta-2`,`Net effect on BP`],rows:[[`Adrenaline (low dose)`,`+`,`+++`,`+++`,`Diastolic falls, mean roughly unchanged`],[`Adrenaline (high dose)`,`+++`,`+++`,`++`,`Systolic and diastolic rise`],[`Noradrenaline`,`+++`,`++`,`0`,`Both rise; reflex bradycardia`],[`Isoprenaline`,`0`,`+++`,`+++`,`Diastolic falls, tachycardia`],[`Phenylephrine`,`+++`,`0`,`0`,`Rise with reflex bradycardia`]],caption:`Reflex bradycardia with noradrenaline and phenylephrine is vagally mediated and abolished by atropine.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`False transmitters`,text:`Methyldopa is converted to alpha-methylnoradrenaline, which is a potent alpha-2 agonist and lowers central sympathetic outflow — an effective false transmitter. Tyramine also enters the vesicle but is a WEAK agonist, so it displaces noradrenaline and causes a pressor surge, the basis of the cheese reaction with MAO inhibitors.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Reserpine blocks VMAT so amine stores are permanently depleted; guanethidine is taken up by uptake-1 and displaces noradrenaline, and its action is abolished by tricyclic antidepressants.`,`Cocaine potentiates directly acting agents (uptake blocked) but reduces the effect of indirectly acting agents such as tyramine, since they need uptake-1 to enter.`,`Terbutaline, ritodrine and isoxsuprine are beta-2 agonists used as uterine relaxants; salmeterol and formoterol are long acting, formoterol having the faster onset.`,`Mirabegron is a beta-3 agonist for overactive bladder, avoiding antimuscarinic dryness.`,`Sotalol is unusual: a non-selective beta blocker that also blocks potassium channels (class III), prolongs QT and can cause torsades de pointes.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Cardioselective beta blockers`,text:`A BEAM of selectivity: Atenolol, Bisoprolol, Esmolol, Acebutolol, Metoprolol. Everything before N in the alphabet (A to M) is broadly the selective group; N onwards (nadolol, oxprenolol, pindolol, propranolol, sotalol, timolol) is non-selective.`}]},{topicId:`ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`A sedative calms without causing sleep; a hypnotic induces sleep. An anxiolytic relieves anxiety. Most of these drugs work by boosting GABA, the main inhibitory transmitter of the brain. Antiepileptics stop the abnormal electrical discharge that causes seizures, usually by damping sodium or calcium channels or by boosting GABA.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`GABA-A receptor: a chloride ion channel. When GABA opens it, chloride enters, the neurone becomes more negative, and firing is suppressed.`,`Benzodiazepines (BZD) such as diazepam, lorazepam, alprazolam bind a separate site on GABA-A and make GABA work better.`,`Barbiturates such as phenobarbitone bind another site on the same channel and can open it even without GABA at high dose.`,`Z-drugs (zolpidem, zopiclone, zaleplon) are non-benzodiazepine hypnotics acting at the same benzodiazepine site.`,`Flumazenil is the specific antidote for benzodiazepine overdose. There is no antidote for barbiturates.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Frequency versus duration`,text:`Benzodiazepines increase the FREQUENCY of chloride channel opening. Barbiturates increase the DURATION of opening. Remember: Ben Frequents the Bar for a long Duration.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`What benzodiazepines are used for`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Anxiety: alprazolam, lorazepam.`,`Insomnia: nitrazepam, temazepam, or the Z-drugs.`,`Status epilepticus: intravenous lorazepam or diazepam.`,`Muscle spasm and spasticity: diazepam.`,`Alcohol withdrawal: chlordiazepoxide or diazepam.`,`Preanaesthetic medication and conscious sedation: midazolam, which also produces anterograde amnesia.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Seizure types in one line each`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Generalised tonic-clonic (grand mal): whole body stiffens then jerks, with loss of consciousness.`,`Absence (petit mal): brief blank stare in a child, no fall, 3 Hz spike-and-wave on EEG.`,`Myoclonic: sudden brief muscle jerks.`,`Focal (partial): starts in one area; may or may not impair awareness.`,`Status epilepticus: a seizure lasting over 5 minutes or repeated seizures without recovery. A medical emergency.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Absence seizure`,text:`Ethosuximide for absence. If the child also has tonic-clonic seizures, use valproate instead, because ethosuximide covers only absence.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Phenytoin, carbamazepine, valproate and phenobarbitone are the older drugs; lamotrigine, levetiracetam, topiramate and gabapentin are newer. Newer does not mean stronger — it usually means fewer interactions and a cleaner side-effect profile.`}]},{topicId:`ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Two tables carry this topic: the benzodiazepine versus barbiturate comparison, and drug of choice by seizure type with the matching mechanism.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Benzodiazepines versus barbiturates`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Benzodiazepines`,`Barbiturates`],rows:[[`Site on GABA-A`,`BZD site (alpha-gamma interface)`,`Barbiturate site`],[`Effect on Cl- channel`,`Increases frequency of opening`,`Increases duration of opening`],[`Action without GABA`,`None (ceiling effect)`,`Direct channel opening at high dose`],[`Dose-response ceiling`,`Yes, hence high safety`,`No ceiling, respiratory depression`],[`Effect on REM sleep`,`Mild suppression`,`Marked suppression, rebound on stopping`],[`Enzyme induction`,`Negligible`,`Strong CYP and glucuronidation inducer`],[`Antidote`,`Flumazenil`,`None; alkaline diuresis and support`],[`Therapeutic index`,`High`,`Low`]],caption:`The absence of a ceiling is why barbiturates kill in overdose and benzodiazepines rarely do when taken alone.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Flumazenil is a competitive BZD-site antagonist, given IV, onset 1-2 min, but its half-life (about 1 hour) is shorter than most benzodiazepines, so resedation occurs and repeat doses are needed.`,`Flumazenil must be avoided in mixed overdose with tricyclic antidepressants and in BZD-dependent patients — it can precipitate seizures.`,`Zolpidem acts selectively at the alpha-1 (BZ1) subtype, giving hypnotic action with little anxiolytic, anticonvulsant or muscle-relaxant effect and less tolerance.`,`Buspirone is a 5-HT1A partial agonist anxiolytic: no sedation, no dependence, but it takes 1-2 weeks to work.`,`Melatonin receptor agonist ramelteon and the orexin antagonist suvorexant are non-GABA hypnotic options.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Antiepileptic mechanisms`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Mechanism`,`Drugs`],rows:[[`Sodium channel inactivation prolonged`,`Phenytoin, carbamazepine, oxcarbazepine, lamotrigine, valproate, zonisamide`],[`T-type calcium channel block in thalamus`,`Ethosuximide, valproate, trimethadione`],[`GABA facilitation at GABA-A`,`Benzodiazepines, phenobarbitone, clobazam`],[`GABA transaminase inhibition`,`Vigabatrin, valproate (weak)`],[`GABA reuptake inhibition`,`Tiagabine`],[`SV2A synaptic vesicle protein binding`,`Levetiracetam, brivaracetam`],[`Glutamate AMPA block`,`Perampanel, topiramate (partly)`],[`Alpha-2-delta subunit of Ca channel`,`Gabapentin, pregabalin`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Absence is a T-party`,text:`Absence seizures arise from T-type calcium currents in thalamic neurones. Ethosuximide blocks T-type calcium channels — the mechanism and the indication travel together.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Drug of choice by seizure type`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Seizure type`,`First choice`,`Alternatives`],rows:[[`Generalised tonic-clonic`,`Valproate (levetiracetam in women of child-bearing age)`,`Phenytoin, carbamazepine, lamotrigine`],[`Focal with or without secondary generalisation`,`Carbamazepine or levetiracetam`,`Lamotrigine, oxcarbazepine, phenytoin`],[`Absence`,`Ethosuximide`,`Valproate, lamotrigine`],[`Myoclonic`,`Valproate`,`Levetiracetam, clonazepam, topiramate`],[`Atonic / Lennox-Gastaut`,`Valproate`,`Lamotrigine, topiramate, rufinamide`],[`Infantile spasms (West syndrome)`,`ACTH or vigabatrin`,`Corticosteroids`],[`Febrile seizure prophylaxis`,`Rectal or oral diazepam`,`Clobazam`],[`Status epilepticus`,`IV lorazepam, then IV fosphenytoin or valproate`,`Levetiracetam, then general anaesthesia`]],caption:`Carbamazepine and phenytoin can WORSEN absence and myoclonic seizures — a favourite negative-marking question.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Do not give carbamazepine in absence`,text:`Carbamazepine, phenytoin, gabapentin and vigabatrin aggravate absence and myoclonic seizures. Valproate and lamotrigine are the broad-spectrum agents that cover almost every type.`}]},{topicId:`ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced marks come from phenytoin kinetics, the adverse-effect signatures that identify a drug from a clinical vignette, and teratogenicity.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Phenytoin: zero-order kinetics`},{kind:`para`,text:`Phenytoin is hydroxylated by CYP2C9 and CYP2C19. These enzymes saturate within the therapeutic range (10-20 mcg/mL), so elimination changes from first order to zero order. Beyond saturation a small dose increase produces a disproportionately large rise in plasma level, and the apparent half-life lengthens from about 12 hours to more than 40 hours.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Rate of elimination = Vmax x C / (Km + C)`,caption:`Michaelis-Menten. Vmax = maximal metabolic rate; Km = concentration at half Vmax; C = plasma concentration. When C is much greater than Km, rate approaches Vmax, a constant amount per hour — zero order.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Phenytoin adverse effects`,text:`Gum hypertrophy, hirsutism, coarsening of facial features, acne, megaloblastic anaemia (folate interference), osteomalacia (vitamin D metabolism), lymphadenopathy, and dose-related nystagmus then ataxia then confusion. IV phenytoin given fast causes hypotension and arrhythmia because of the propylene glycol vehicle, so use fosphenytoin.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug`,`Signature adverse effect`,`Other notes`],rows:[[`Valproate`,`Hepatotoxicity, weight gain, alopecia, tremor`,`Highest teratogen risk: neural tube defects; enzyme INHIBITOR`],[`Carbamazepine`,`Hyponatraemia (SIADH), diplopia, agranulocytosis`,`Auto-induces its own metabolism; HLA-B*1502 and SJS`],[`Lamotrigine`,`Serious rash / Stevens-Johnson syndrome`,`Titrate slowly, especially with valproate`],[`Ethosuximide`,`Nausea, hiccups, lupus-like reaction`,`Only for absence`],[`Levetiracetam`,`Irritability, mood change, behavioural upset`,`Renal excretion, no interactions`],[`Topiramate`,`Weight loss, renal stones, word-finding difficulty, glaucoma`,`Carbonic anhydrase inhibition`],[`Vigabatrin`,`Irreversible visual field constriction`,`Reserved for infantile spasms`],[`Phenobarbitone`,`Sedation, paradoxical hyperactivity in children`,`Strong enzyme inducer`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Valproate plus lamotrigine`,text:`Valproate INHIBITS the glucuronidation of lamotrigine and roughly doubles its level, sharply raising the risk of Stevens-Johnson syndrome. The lamotrigine starting dose must be halved. Almost every other antiepileptic is an inducer that lowers lamotrigine.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Fetal hydantoin syndrome: cleft lip and palate, microcephaly, hypoplastic nails and distal phalanges, growth retardation.`,`Valproate in pregnancy causes spina bifida in about 1-2 percent; give folic acid 5 mg daily to any woman of childbearing age on antiepileptics.`,`Antiepileptics that induce CYP reduce the effect of oral contraceptives — an ADR type F (therapeutic failure).`,`Withdrawal of an antiepileptic should be gradual over months and only after 2-3 seizure-free years.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Status epilepticus protocol`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Airway, oxygen, IV access, check blood glucose; thiamine before glucose in alcoholics.`,`IV lorazepam 4 mg (preferred, longer anticonvulsant action than diazepam because it redistributes less) or IV diazepam 10 mg.`,`If seizures persist beyond 5-10 minutes give IV fosphenytoin 20 mg PE/kg, or IV valproate or levetiracetam.`,`Refractory status: general anaesthesia with midazolam infusion, propofol or thiopentone, with intubation and EEG monitoring.`]}]},{topicId:`ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Antipsychotics (neuroleptics) treat schizophrenia and mania; they mostly block dopamine D2 receptors. Antidepressants treat depression; they mostly raise serotonin or noradrenaline in the synapse. Understanding which dopamine pathway is blocked explains both the benefit and every side effect.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The four dopamine pathways`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Pathway`,`From - to`,`Blocking D2 here gives`],rows:[[`Mesolimbic`,`VTA to limbic system`,`Relief of hallucinations and delusions (the benefit)`],[`Mesocortical`,`VTA to prefrontal cortex`,`Worse apathy and blunting (negative symptoms)`],[`Nigrostriatal`,`Substantia nigra to striatum`,`Extrapyramidal side effects (parkinsonism)`],[`Tuberoinfundibular`,`Hypothalamus to pituitary`,`Raised prolactin, galactorrhoea, amenorrhoea`]],caption:`Only the mesolimbic block is wanted. The other three produce the classic adverse effects.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Positive symptoms: hallucinations, delusions, disordered thought. These respond well to D2 blockade.`,`Negative symptoms: flat affect, poverty of speech, social withdrawal. These respond poorly, and better to atypical drugs.`,`Typical (first generation): chlorpromazine, haloperidol, fluphenazine, trifluoperazine.`,`Atypical (second generation): clozapine, olanzapine, risperidone, quetiapine, aripiprazole, ziprasidone.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Why atypicals cause fewer movement problems`,text:`Atypicals block 5-HT2A as well as D2, and bind D2 more loosely. Serotonin normally inhibits dopamine release in the striatum, so blocking 5-HT2A lets dopamine rise there and protects against extrapyramidal effects.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Antidepressants in one line each`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`SSRI (fluoxetine, sertraline, escitalopram): block serotonin reuptake. First-line, safest in overdose.`,`SNRI (venlafaxine, duloxetine): block both serotonin and noradrenaline reuptake.`,`TCA (amitriptyline, imipramine): block both reuptakes but also block muscarinic, H1 and alpha-1 receptors, hence many side effects.`,`MAOI (phenelzine, tranylcypromine, moclobemide): stop the enzyme that destroys the amines.`,`Atypical (mirtazapine, bupropion, trazodone): mixed receptor actions.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Antidepressants take weeks`,text:`Reuptake is blocked within hours but mood improves only after 2-4 weeks, because the benefit needs down-regulation of postsynaptic receptors and neuroplastic change. A patient who says the drug is not working after 5 days has not failed treatment.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Lithium is used for bipolar disorder. It is a simple ion, excreted entirely by the kidney, and has a very narrow therapeutic range, so blood levels must be measured regularly.`}]},{topicId:`ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`This level covers typical versus atypical comparison, the timeline of extrapyramidal reactions with their management, and the adverse-effect signature that identifies each antidepressant class.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Typical`,`Atypical`],rows:[[`Main receptor`,`D2 (tight, high occupancy)`,`D2 loose plus 5-HT2A block`],[`Positive symptoms`,`Good control`,`Good control`],[`Negative symptoms`,`Poor, may worsen`,`Better`],[`Extrapyramidal effects`,`Frequent`,`Much less`],[`Prolactin rise`,`Marked (except aripiprazole class)`,`Less, but risperidone raises it`],[`Metabolic effects`,`Less`,`Weight gain, dyslipidaemia, diabetes (clozapine, olanzapine)`],[`Example`,`Haloperidol, chlorpromazine, fluphenazine`,`Clozapine, olanzapine, risperidone, aripiprazole`]],caption:`Risperidone is the atypical that behaves like a typical at higher doses — EPS and hyperprolactinaemia return.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Extrapyramidal side effects by time of onset`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Reaction`,`Onset`,`Picture`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`Acute dystonia`,`Hours to days`,`Torticollis, oculogyric crisis, trismus`,`IM promethazine or benztropine / trihexyphenidyl`],[`Akathisia`,`Days to weeks`,`Inner restlessness, cannot sit still`,`Propranolol; reduce dose; benzodiazepine`],[`Parkinsonism`,`Weeks to months`,`Rigidity, bradykinesia, tremor`,`Anticholinergic; switch to atypical. Never levodopa`],[`Tardive dyskinesia`,`Months to years`,`Orofacial chewing, lip smacking, choreoathetosis`,`Stop or switch to clozapine; valbenazine; anticholinergics WORSEN it`]],caption:`Order of appearance: dystonia, akathisia, parkinsonism, tardive dyskinesia — earliest to latest.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`ADAPT`,text:`Acute Dystonia, Akathisia, Parkinsonism, Tardive dyskinesia — in that chronological order. Anticholinergics help the first three and worsen the last.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS)`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Tetrad: hyperthermia, lead-pipe muscle rigidity, autonomic instability, altered consciousness.`,`Laboratory: raised creatine kinase, leucocytosis, myoglobinuria, risk of acute kidney injury.`,`Most often with high-potency typicals such as haloperidol, especially on rapid dose escalation.`,`Treatment: stop the antipsychotic, cool actively, give dantrolene (muscle relaxant) and bromocriptine or amantadine (dopamine agonists), supportive care.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Clozapine`,text:`Clozapine is the only agent proven effective in treatment-resistant schizophrenia and it reduces suicidality, but it causes agranulocytosis in 1-2 percent. Weekly then monthly white cell counts are mandatory. It also causes seizures, myocarditis, sialorrhoea (paradoxical drooling) and marked weight gain, and it has the LEAST extrapyramidal effect.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Antidepressant classes and their signatures`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Example`,`Mechanism`,`Distinctive adverse effect`],rows:[[`SSRI`,`Fluoxetine, sertraline`,`Serotonin reuptake block`,`Sexual dysfunction, GI upset, hyponatraemia, initial anxiety`],[`SNRI`,`Venlafaxine, duloxetine`,`Serotonin and NA reuptake block`,`Dose-related rise in blood pressure`],[`TCA`,`Amitriptyline, imipramine`,`Serotonin and NA reuptake block`,`Anticholinergic, postural hypotension, cardiotoxic in overdose`],[`MAOI (irreversible)`,`Phenelzine, tranylcypromine`,`MAO-A and B inhibition`,`Cheese reaction, hepatotoxicity`],[`RIMA`,`Moclobemide`,`Reversible MAO-A inhibition`,`Much less tyramine risk`],[`NaSSA`,`Mirtazapine`,`Alpha-2 block, 5-HT2 and 5-HT3 block`,`Sedation, marked weight gain, but no sexual dysfunction`],[`NDRI`,`Bupropion`,`NA and dopamine reuptake block`,`Seizures, insomnia; used for smoking cessation`],[`SARI`,`Trazodone`,`5-HT2 block plus weak reuptake block`,`Sedation, priapism`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Fluoxetine is the long one`,text:`Fluoxetine and its active metabolite norfluoxetine have half-lives of days to weeks, so it causes the least discontinuation syndrome but needs the longest washout (5 weeks) before starting an MAOI. Paroxetine and venlafaxine, with short half-lives, cause the worst withdrawal.`}]},{topicId:`ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced questions test the emergencies — serotonin syndrome, the cheese reaction, TCA overdose and lithium toxicity — and the receptor detail behind individual drug choices.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Serotonin syndrome`,`Neuroleptic malignant syndrome`],rows:[[`Onset`,`Within 24 hours`,`Days to weeks`],[`Neuromuscular sign`,`Clonus, hyperreflexia, myoclonus`,`Lead-pipe rigidity, hyporeflexia`],[`Pupils`,`Dilated`,`Normal`],[`Bowel sounds`,`Hyperactive, diarrhoea`,`Normal or reduced`],[`Trigger`,`Serotonergic drug added`,`Dopamine blocker started or increased`],[`Specific treatment`,`Cyproheptadine (5-HT2 blocker)`,`Dantrolene plus bromocriptine`]],caption:`Both give hyperthermia and autonomic instability; the muscle findings and pupils separate them.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Serotonin syndrome is precipitated by combining SSRI with MAOI, tramadol, pethidine, linezolid, triptans, St John wort or dextromethorphan.`,`Allow a 2-week washout between an MAOI and any serotonergic drug, and 5 weeks after fluoxetine.`,`Cheese reaction: an irreversible MAO inhibitor lets dietary tyramine (aged cheese, red wine, yeast extract, pickled herring, broad beans) escape gut MAO-A, enter noradrenergic terminals and displace stored noradrenaline, giving a hypertensive crisis with throbbing headache. Treat with phentolamine.`,`Moclobemide is reversible and MAO-A selective, so tyramine displaces it from the enzyme and the risk is small.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`TCA overdose: the three Cs`,text:`Coma, Convulsions and Cardiotoxicity. Sodium channel block widens QRS beyond 100 ms and causes ventricular arrhythmia. Treatment is sodium bicarbonate to alkalinise plasma and raise the sodium gradient, plus supportive care. Physostigmine and flumazenil are contraindicated.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Lithium`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Therapeutic plasma level 0.6-1.2 mEq/L; toxicity above 1.5 mEq/L; life-threatening above 2.5 mEq/L. One of the narrowest therapeutic indices in medicine.`,`Mechanism: inhibits inositol monophosphatase, depleting the phosphatidylinositol second messenger pool, and inhibits GSK-3.`,`Adverse effects: fine tremor, polyuria from nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, hypothyroidism and goitre, weight gain, acne, leucocytosis, and Ebstein anomaly if given in the first trimester.`,`Toxicity signs: coarse tremor, vomiting, diarrhoea, ataxia, dysarthria, confusion, then seizures and coma. Treat with saline and haemodialysis; there is no antidote.`,`Levels RISE with thiazides, NSAIDs (except aspirin and sulindac), ACE inhibitors, dehydration and salt restriction. Levels FALL with osmotic diuretics, theophylline and sodium loading.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Thiazide plus lithium`,text:`Thiazides cause sodium loss, so the proximal tubule reabsorbs more sodium and, with it, more lithium — the level rises and toxicity follows. Loop diuretics and amiloride do this much less.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Chlorpromazine typically causes sedation, postural hypotension, cholestatic jaundice, photosensitivity and corneal deposits; thioridazine prolongs QT and causes retinal pigmentation; haloperidol has the highest EPS and least autonomic effect.`,`Aripiprazole is a D2 PARTIAL agonist (dopamine system stabiliser), producing antipsychotic action with low prolactin and low EPS.`,`Quetiapine has the lowest EPS after clozapine and is preferred for psychosis in Parkinson disease, along with clozapine and pimavanserin.`,`Depot preparations (fluphenazine decanoate, haloperidol decanoate, paliperidone palmitate) are given every 2-4 weeks for poor adherence.`,`Electroconvulsive therapy remains the fastest treatment for severe depression with suicidal risk or catatonia.`]}]},{topicId:`ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`This topic groups three CNS families: opioid analgesics that kill severe pain, anaesthetics that abolish sensation for surgery, and antiparkinsonian drugs that restore brain dopamine.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Opioid receptors`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Receptor`,`Effects when activated`,`Endogenous ligand`],rows:[[`Mu (MOP)`,`Analgesia, euphoria, respiratory depression, constipation, miosis, dependence`,`Beta-endorphin`],[`Kappa (KOP)`,`Spinal analgesia, sedation, dysphoria, miosis, less dependence`,`Dynorphin`],[`Delta (DOP)`,`Analgesia, mood modulation`,`Enkephalins`]],caption:`All three are Gi-coupled GPCRs: they lower cAMP, open potassium channels and close calcium channels, reducing transmitter release.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Morphine is the standard. It relieves severe pain, causes euphoria, sedation, pinpoint pupils, constipation, and slows breathing.`,`Codeine is a weak opioid, useful for cough and mild pain; it is a prodrug converted to morphine by CYP2D6.`,`Pethidine and fentanyl are synthetic; fentanyl is about 100 times more potent than morphine.`,`Tramadol is a weak mu agonist that also blocks serotonin and noradrenaline reuptake.`,`Naloxone is the injectable antidote that reverses opioid overdose within minutes.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Opioid overdose triad`,text:`Pinpoint pupils, respiratory depression and coma. Add needle marks and the diagnosis is certain. Give naloxone IV — the pupils dilate and breathing returns within 1-2 minutes.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`General anaesthesia`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Stage I Analgesia: consciousness retained, pain sense dulled.`,`Stage II Excitement (delirium): loss of consciousness with struggling, irregular breathing, dilated pupils. Dangerous; pass through it quickly.`,`Stage III Surgical anaesthesia: regular breathing, loss of reflexes, muscle relaxation. Divided into four planes.`,`Stage IV Medullary paralysis: respiratory and vasomotor collapse. Fatal unless reversed at once.`]},{kind:`para`,text:`A local anaesthetic blocks sodium channels in a nerve so that impulses cannot travel past the blocked segment. Lignocaine is the standard. Adrenaline is often added to constrict local vessels, keep the drug in place, prolong the block and reduce bleeding.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Parkinson disease is caused by loss of dopamine neurones in the substantia nigra. Levodopa, a dopamine precursor that crosses into the brain, is the most effective treatment. It is always combined with carbidopa so that it is not destroyed before reaching the brain.`}]},{topicId:`ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The examinable core is the opioid agonist-antagonist spectrum, MAC and blood-gas solubility for inhalational agents, the amide versus ester split for local anaesthetics, and the antiparkinsonian classification.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Opioid classification`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Group`,`Drugs`,`Receptor profile`],rows:[[`Strong agonist`,`Morphine, pethidine, fentanyl, methadone`,`Full mu agonist`],[`Moderate / weak agonist`,`Codeine, dihydrocodeine, tramadol`,`Partial or weak mu`],[`Mixed agonist-antagonist`,`Pentazocine, nalbuphine, butorphanol`,`Kappa agonist, mu antagonist or partial`],[`Partial agonist`,`Buprenorphine`,`Mu partial agonist, kappa antagonist`],[`Pure antagonist`,`Naloxone, naltrexone, nalmefene`,`Blocks mu, kappa, delta`]],caption:`Giving pentazocine or buprenorphine to a morphine-dependent patient precipitates withdrawal, because they displace morphine from mu receptors.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Tolerance develops to analgesia, euphoria, sedation, respiratory depression and emesis. It does NOT develop to miosis or constipation.`,`Morphine releases histamine, causing itching, urticaria and bronchospasm; it is avoided in asthma and head injury (CO2 retention raises intracranial pressure).`,`Pethidine has an antimuscarinic action so it does not cause miosis, and its metabolite norpethidine is convulsant, accumulating in renal failure.`,`Methadone has a long half-life and is used for maintenance and detoxification.`,`Morphine causes biliary spasm through the sphincter of Oddi, so pethidine has traditionally been preferred in biliary colic.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Naloxone versus naltrexone`,text:`Naloxone is parenteral, acts in 1-2 minutes and lasts only 1-2 hours, so a long-acting opioid can outlast it and the patient re-sedates. Naltrexone is orally active and lasts 24-48 hours, so it is used for maintenance of abstinence in opioid and alcohol dependence, not for acute overdose.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Inhalational anaesthetics`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Property`,`What it means`,`Consequence`],rows:[[`MAC`,`Alveolar concentration at which 50 percent do not move to surgical incision`,`Low MAC = high potency`],[`Blood-gas partition coefficient`,`Solubility in blood`,`Low value = fast induction and recovery`],[`Oil-gas partition coefficient`,`Lipid solubility`,`High value = high potency, inversely related to MAC`]],caption:`MAC measures potency; blood-gas coefficient measures speed. They are independent properties.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Agent`,`MAC (percent)`,`Blood-gas coefficient`,`Note`],rows:[[`Nitrous oxide`,`Over 100`,`0.47`,`Weak anaesthetic, good analgesic, fastest`],[`Desflurane`,`6.0`,`0.42`,`Fastest recovery; airway irritant`],[`Sevoflurane`,`2.0`,`0.65`,`Smooth, pleasant induction; paediatric choice`],[`Isoflurane`,`1.2`,`1.4`,`Most widely used maintenance agent`],[`Halothane`,`0.75`,`2.3`,`Potent, slow; hepatitis and arrhythmia risk`],[`Ether`,`1.9`,`12`,`Obsolete; very slow, flammable, good relaxation`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Second gas effect`,text:`A high concentration of nitrous oxide is taken up so fast that it concentrates the second gas left behind in the alveoli, speeding its uptake. On stopping, the reverse (diffusion hypoxia) occurs, so give 100 percent oxygen for a few minutes after discontinuing nitrous oxide.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Local anaesthetics`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Ester`,`Amide`],rows:[[`Examples`,`Procaine, cocaine, benzocaine, tetracaine`,`Lignocaine, bupivacaine, ropivacaine, prilocaine`],[`Metabolism`,`Plasma pseudocholinesterase`,`Hepatic CYP (dealkylation)`],[`Duration`,`Short`,`Longer`],[`Allergy`,`More common (PABA metabolite)`,`Rare`],[`Spelling clue`,`One i in the name`,`Two i letters in the name`]],caption:`Count the letter i: lignocaine and bupivacaine have two, procaine has one.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Why local anaesthetics fail in inflamed tissue`,text:`Local anaesthetics are weak bases with pKa around 8. Inflamed tissue is acidic, so a far larger fraction is ionised. Only the unionised form crosses the nerve membrane, so less drug gets in and the block fails. Once inside, it is the ionised (cationic) form that blocks the sodium channel from the inner side.`}]},{topicId:`ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced items: malignant hyperthermia, the adrenaline-with-local-anaesthetic rules, differential nerve block and the fine detail of levodopa therapy.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Malignant hyperthermia`,text:`An autosomal dominant ryanodine receptor (RYR1) defect. Succinylcholine or a halogenated agent (halothane, sevoflurane, isoflurane) triggers uncontrolled calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum: masseter spasm, rigidity, a rapid rise in end-tidal CO2, hyperthermia, acidosis and rhabdomyolysis. Treatment is dantrolene 2.5 mg/kg IV, which blocks the ryanodine receptor, plus cooling and stopping the trigger.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Adrenaline 1 in 200,000 added to a local anaesthetic prolongs the block, lowers systemic toxicity and reduces bleeding.`,`It must NOT be used in fingers, toes, nose, ear lobes or penis (end-arteries) because vasoconstriction can cause gangrene, nor with cocaine, nor in thyrotoxicosis or severe hypertension.`,`Cocaine is the only local anaesthetic that is itself a vasoconstrictor, because it blocks noradrenaline uptake-1.`,`Order of blockade: autonomic fibres first, then pain and temperature, then touch, then pressure, then motor. Recovery is in reverse.`,`Small unmyelinated C and small myelinated A-delta fibres are blocked before large A-alpha motor fibres.`,`Bupivacaine is the most cardiotoxic local anaesthetic (persistent sodium channel block); intravenous lipid emulsion is the rescue therapy.`,`Prilocaine and benzocaine cause methaemoglobinaemia, treated with methylene blue.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Intravenous anaesthetics`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Thiopentone: ultrashort-acting barbiturate, action ends by redistribution to muscle and fat, not metabolism. No analgesia. Contraindicated in porphyria.`,`Propofol: rapid, smooth, antiemetic, ideal for day-care surgery; causes pain on injection and hypotension.`,`Ketamine: dissociative anaesthesia by NMDA block, gives analgesia, raises blood pressure and preserves airway reflexes; causes emergence delirium prevented by a benzodiazepine.`,`Etomidate: cardiostable, so used in shock; suppresses adrenal steroidogenesis.`,`Midazolam and fentanyl are the usual adjuncts for sedation and analgesia.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Maximum safe dose of lignocaine = 4.5 mg/kg plain, 7 mg/kg with adrenaline`,caption:`A 70 kg adult may receive about 315 mg plain, i.e. roughly 31 mL of 1 percent solution (1 percent = 10 mg/mL).`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Antiparkinsonian drugs`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Drugs`,`Point to remember`],rows:[[`Dopamine precursor`,`Levodopa with carbidopa or benserazide`,`Most effective; peripheral decarboxylase inhibitor cuts the dose by about 75 percent`],[`Dopamine agonist`,`Pramipexole, ropinirole, bromocriptine, rotigotine, apomorphine`,`First choice in the young; causes impulse control disorder`],[`MAO-B inhibitor`,`Selegiline, rasagiline`,`Mild benefit, spares dopamine; selegiline yields amphetamine metabolites`],[`COMT inhibitor`,`Entacapone, tolcapone`,`Only useful WITH levodopa; tolcapone is hepatotoxic`],[`Anticholinergic`,`Trihexyphenidyl, benztropine, biperiden`,`Best for tremor and for drug-induced parkinsonism`],[`Others`,`Amantadine`,`Releases dopamine and blocks NMDA; helps levodopa dyskinesia`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Carbidopa does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so it blocks DOPA decarboxylase only in the periphery, reducing nausea, hypotension and arrhythmia and increasing brain delivery.`,`Pyridoxine (vitamin B6) enhances peripheral decarboxylation and abolishes the effect of levodopa given alone — but not when carbidopa is present.`,`Long-term levodopa causes the on-off phenomenon, end-of-dose wearing off and peak-dose dyskinesia.`,`Levodopa is contraindicated in narrow-angle glaucoma and melanoma, and must not be combined with a non-selective MAO inhibitor.`,`Drug-induced parkinsonism is treated with an anticholinergic, NEVER with levodopa, because the receptors are blocked.`,`Apomorphine, a potent dopamine agonist, is given subcutaneously as rescue for sudden off periods, with domperidone to prevent vomiting.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Metoclopramide versus domperidone`,text:`Both are dopamine antagonists, but domperidone does not cross the blood-brain barrier appreciably, so it controls levodopa-induced vomiting without worsening parkinsonism. Metoclopramide crosses and would aggravate the disease.`}]},{topicId:`ph-cvs-antihypertensives`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Blood pressure is cardiac output multiplied by peripheral resistance. Every antihypertensive lowers one or both. Angina is chest pain caused by the heart muscle receiving less oxygen than it needs; antianginal drugs either increase supply or reduce demand.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`BP = Cardiac output x Total peripheral resistance`,caption:`BP = arterial blood pressure; cardiac output = stroke volume x heart rate; TPR is set mainly by arteriolar tone.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The main groups`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Diuretics (hydrochlorothiazide, chlorthalidone): remove salt and water, lower blood volume.`,`ACE inhibitors (enalapril, ramipril, lisinopril): stop angiotensin II being formed, so vessels dilate.`,`ARBs (losartan, telmisartan, valsartan): block the angiotensin II receptor instead.`,`Calcium channel blockers (amlodipine, nifedipine, verapamil, diltiazem): relax arteriolar smooth muscle.`,`Beta blockers (metoprolol, atenolol): reduce heart rate, force and renin release.`,`Alpha-1 blockers (prazosin) and central agents (clonidine, methyldopa) are used in special situations.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The ACE inhibitor cough`,text:`ACE also destroys bradykinin. Blocking ACE lets bradykinin and substance P accumulate in the airway, causing a persistent dry cough in 10-20 percent and, rarely, angioedema. ARBs do not touch bradykinin, so they do not cause cough — switch the patient to an ARB.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Angina in one line each`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Stable (classical, exertional): pain on exertion, relieved by rest. Caused by a fixed atheromatous narrowing.`,`Unstable: pain at rest or of increasing severity. A plaque has ruptured; this is an emergency.`,`Variant (Prinzmetal): pain at rest from coronary artery SPASM, often at night, with ST elevation.`]},{kind:`para`,text:`Glyceryl trinitrate (GTN) placed under the tongue relieves an anginal attack in 1-2 minutes. It is given sublingually because swallowing it would destroy almost all of it in the liver on first pass. Headache and flushing are its usual side effects.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Never give a nitrate with sildenafil`,text:`Both raise cyclic GMP — the nitrate makes it and sildenafil stops it being broken down. Together they cause severe, potentially fatal hypotension. Allow at least 24 hours after sildenafil and 48 hours after tadalafil.`}]},{topicId:`ph-cvs-antihypertensives`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`You need the full classification, the comorbidity-based drug of choice table, and the mechanism and tolerance behaviour of nitrates.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Classification of antihypertensives`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Subgroup`,`Examples`],rows:[[`Diuretics`,`Thiazide / thiazide-like`,`Hydrochlorothiazide, chlorthalidone, indapamide`],[`Diuretics`,`Loop; potassium sparing`,`Frusemide; spironolactone, amiloride`],[`RAAS blockers`,`ACE inhibitors`,`Captopril, enalapril, ramipril, lisinopril`],[`RAAS blockers`,`ARBs; renin inhibitor`,`Losartan, telmisartan, valsartan; aliskiren`],[`Calcium channel blockers`,`Dihydropyridine`,`Amlodipine, nifedipine, felodipine, nimodipine`],[`Calcium channel blockers`,`Non-dihydropyridine`,`Verapamil (phenylalkylamine), diltiazem (benzothiazepine)`],[`Sympatholytics`,`Beta blockers; alpha blockers`,`Atenolol, metoprolol; prazosin`],[`Sympatholytics`,`Central alpha-2 agonists`,`Clonidine, methyldopa, guanfacine`],[`Vasodilators`,`Arteriolar; arteriovenous`,`Hydralazine, minoxidil, diazoxide; sodium nitroprusside`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`CCB selectivity`,text:`Dihydropyridines act on Vessels (amlodipine, nifedipine); verapamil acts on the Ventricle (heart) most; diltiazem sits in between. So dihydropyridines cause reflex tachycardia and ankle oedema, while verapamil causes bradycardia and constipation.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Comorbidity`,`Preferred drug`,`Avoid`],rows:[[`Diabetes or proteinuric CKD`,`ACE inhibitor or ARB`,`High-dose thiazide, non-selective beta blocker`],[`Pregnancy`,`Labetalol, methyldopa, nifedipine`,`ACE inhibitors, ARBs, aliskiren (fetotoxic)`],[`Bronchial asthma`,`CCB, ARB, diuretic`,`Beta blockers`],[`Post-myocardial infarction`,`Beta blocker plus ACE inhibitor`,`Short-acting nifedipine`],[`Heart failure`,`ACE inhibitor, beta blocker, spironolactone`,`Verapamil, diltiazem`],[`Benign prostatic hyperplasia`,`Alpha-1 blocker (prazosin, terazosin)`,`-`],[`Elderly, isolated systolic hypertension`,`CCB or thiazide-like diuretic`,`-`],[`Gout`,`ARB (losartan is uricosuric)`,`Thiazide and loop diuretics`]],caption:`Methyldopa is the most extensively studied agent in pregnancy; labetalol is the usual first choice in current practice.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Hypertensive emergency`,text:`Sodium nitroprusside IV is the classic agent: it dilates arteries and veins, acts within seconds and stops within minutes. Cyanide accumulates on prolonged infusion, treated with sodium thiosulphate or hydroxocobalamin. Alternatives are labetalol, esmolol, nicardipine and, in phaeochromocytoma, phentolamine. Lower the pressure by no more than 25 percent in the first hour.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Nitrates`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`The nitrate is denitrated, mainly by mitochondrial aldehyde dehydrogenase (ALDH2), releasing nitric oxide.`,`Nitric oxide activates soluble guanylyl cyclase, raising cyclic GMP.`,`Cyclic GMP activates protein kinase G, which dephosphorylates myosin light chain.`,`Smooth muscle relaxes — veins much more than arteries at usual doses.`,`Venodilation lowers preload and therefore wall tension and myocardial oxygen demand. This, not coronary dilation, is the main antianginal action.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug`,`Route`,`Onset`,`Duration`,`Use`],rows:[[`Glyceryl trinitrate`,`Sublingual`,`1-2 min`,`20-30 min`,`Acute attack`],[`Isosorbide dinitrate`,`Sublingual / oral`,`2-5 min / 30 min`,`1 h / 4-6 h`,`Attack / prophylaxis`],[`Isosorbide mononitrate`,`Oral`,`30-60 min`,`6-10 h`,`Prophylaxis; 100 percent bioavailable`],[`GTN transdermal patch`,`Skin`,`30-60 min`,`10-12 h worn`,`Prophylaxis, remove at night`]],caption:`Isosorbide mononitrate is the active metabolite of the dinitrate and escapes first-pass metabolism entirely.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Nitrate tolerance`,text:`Continuous exposure depletes tissue thiol (sulfhydryl) groups and impairs ALDH2, so the response fades within 24 hours. The remedy is a nitrate-free interval of 8-12 hours, usually overnight. Note the paradox: an angina patient needs the drug at night in variant angina, so timing must be reversed there.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Variant angina`,text:`Variant (Prinzmetal) angina is coronary SPASM. Calcium channel blockers and nitrates are the treatment. Beta blockers are avoided because blocking beta-2 mediated coronary dilation leaves alpha-1 vasoconstriction unopposed and can worsen the spasm.`}]},{topicId:`ph-cvs-antihypertensives`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced marks come from the fine points of RAAS pharmacology, drug-specific adverse-effect signatures, and second-line antianginals.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`ACE (kininase II) both makes angiotensin II and destroys bradykinin, which is why one enzyme produces two unrelated effects.`,`Captopril is the only ACE inhibitor with a sulfhydryl group; it causes rash and taste disturbance (dysgeusia) and needs 2-3 daily doses. Lisinopril is the only one that is not a prodrug and is not metabolised.`,`All ACE inhibitors and ARBs are contraindicated in bilateral renal artery stenosis, pregnancy and hyperkalaemia. They raise serum creatinine and potassium.`,`Angioedema with an ACE inhibitor contraindicates the whole class; ARBs carry a small cross-reactivity risk.`,`Aliskiren is a direct renin inhibitor; combining it with an ACE inhibitor or ARB is contraindicated because of renal failure and hyperkalaemia.`,`Sacubitril, a neprilysin inhibitor, is combined with valsartan (ARNI) for heart failure; it must never be given with an ACE inhibitor because of angioedema risk.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug`,`Signature adverse effect`],rows:[[`Hydralazine`,`Drug-induced lupus (slow acetylators), reflex tachycardia`],[`Minoxidil`,`Hypertrichosis, pericardial effusion, marked salt retention`],[`Clonidine`,`Sedation, dry mouth, severe rebound hypertension on abrupt stop`],[`Methyldopa`,`Positive Coombs test, haemolytic anaemia, hepatitis, sedation`],[`Nifedipine (short acting)`,`Reflex tachycardia, flushing, ankle oedema, gum hyperplasia`],[`Verapamil`,`Constipation, bradycardia, AV block, worsens heart failure`],[`Sodium nitroprusside`,`Cyanide and thiocyanate toxicity, photodegradation`],[`Spironolactone`,`Gynaecomastia, hyperkalaemia`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Clonidine withdrawal`,text:`Abrupt withdrawal of clonidine causes a surge of sympathetic outflow with severe rebound hypertension, agitation and sweating. It is worst if the patient is also on a beta blocker, because unopposed alpha stimulation results. Withdraw the beta blocker first, or taper clonidine over a week.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Nimodipine is the dihydropyridine that preferentially reaches cerebral vessels and is used to limit vasospasm after subarachnoid haemorrhage.`,`Verapamil and diltiazem must never be combined with a beta blocker intravenously — the additive negative chronotropic and dromotropic effect can cause asystole.`,`Ranolazine inhibits the late inward sodium current, reducing calcium overload; it improves angina without lowering heart rate or blood pressure.`,`Ivabradine slows the sinus node funny (If) current and is used in stable angina when beta blockers are not tolerated; it causes luminous phenomena (phosphenes).`,`Trimetazidine shifts myocardial metabolism from fatty acid to glucose oxidation, a metabolic antianginal.`,`Nicorandil is a potassium channel opener with a nitrate moiety, giving both arterial and venous dilation; it can cause oral and gastrointestinal ulceration.`,`Dipyridamole causes coronary steal and must NOT be used in angina, though it is a useful antiplatelet and stress-test agent.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Grapefruit juice`,text:`Grapefruit juice inhibits intestinal CYP3A4 and raises the bioavailability of felodipine, nifedipine and other dihydropyridines, producing exaggerated hypotension. It is the classic food-drug interaction question.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Myocardial oxygen demand is proportional to heart rate x systolic BP (rate-pressure product)`,caption:`Rate-pressure product is the bedside index of oxygen demand; beta blockers lower both terms, which is why they raise the anginal threshold.`}]},{topicId:`ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`In heart failure the heart cannot pump enough blood for the body. Treatment either makes the heart pump harder (inotropes) or reduces the load it has to pump against and the amount of fluid it holds. An arrhythmia is an abnormal heart rhythm; antiarrhythmics act on the ion channels that generate the heartbeat.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Preload: the volume of blood returning to the heart. Diuretics and nitrates reduce it.`,`Afterload: the resistance the heart pumps against. ACE inhibitors, ARBs and vasodilators reduce it.`,`Inotropic effect: change in force of contraction. Positive inotropes strengthen it.`,`Chronotropic effect: change in heart rate. Dromotropic: change in conduction velocity.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Digoxin, the classic cardiac glycoside`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Digoxin blocks the sodium-potassium ATPase pump in the heart muscle cell membrane.`,`Sodium therefore builds up inside the cell.`,`The sodium-calcium exchanger works less well, so calcium also builds up inside.`,`More calcium is stored in the sarcoplasmic reticulum and released with each beat.`,`Contraction becomes stronger — a positive inotropic effect.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Digoxin does two opposite things`,text:`It strengthens contraction directly (positive inotropic) but SLOWS the heart and AV conduction through increased vagal tone (negative chronotropic and dromotropic). That is why it controls the ventricular rate in atrial fibrillation.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`What the heart cells use to beat`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Phase 0: fast sodium entry causes rapid depolarisation of atrial and ventricular cells.`,`Phase 1 and 2: potassium leaves briefly, then calcium enters, holding the plateau.`,`Phase 3: potassium leaves and the cell repolarises.`,`Phase 4: slow leak brings the pacemaker cell back to threshold — this is automaticity.`,`SA and AV nodal cells depolarise mainly by CALCIUM, not sodium, which is why verapamil works on them.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Vaughan Williams in one line`,text:`Class I blocks Sodium, Class II is Beta blockers, Class III blocks Potassium, Class IV blocks Calcium. Some Block Potassium Channels: Sodium, Beta, Potassium, Calcium.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Amiodarone is the broadest antiarrhythmic and is effective in almost every rhythm disturbance, but it has more long-term toxicity than any other cardiac drug. Adenosine, given as a rapid IV push, stops paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia within seconds and is gone in under 10 seconds.`}]},{topicId:`ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Two systems dominate: the modern heart-failure drug hierarchy (which drugs prolong life versus which only relieve symptoms) and the Vaughan Williams classification with a named example per subclass.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Drugs in chronic heart failure`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Examples`,`Effect on survival`],rows:[[`ACE inhibitor / ARB / ARNI`,`Enalapril, ramipril; valsartan; sacubitril-valsartan`,`Improves survival - cornerstone`],[`Beta blocker`,`Metoprolol succinate, bisoprolol, carvedilol, nebivolol`,`Improves survival; start low, go slow`],[`Mineralocorticoid antagonist`,`Spironolactone, eplerenone`,`Improves survival`],[`SGLT2 inhibitor`,`Dapagliflozin, empagliflozin`,`Improves survival even without diabetes`],[`Loop diuretic`,`Frusemide, torsemide`,`Symptom relief only`],[`Digoxin`,`Digoxin`,`Reduces hospitalisation, no survival benefit`],[`Ivabradine`,`Ivabradine`,`Benefit if sinus rate stays above 70 per minute`],[`Vasodilator`,`Hydralazine plus isosorbide dinitrate`,`Benefit when ACE inhibitors cannot be used`]],caption:`Only the first four classes prolong life. Diuretics and digoxin make patients feel better without extending life.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Beta blockers in heart failure`,text:`It sounds contradictory to give a negative inotrope in a failing heart, but chronic sympathetic overdrive is what destroys myocardium. Only bisoprolol, carvedilol, metoprolol succinate and nebivolol are proven, must be started at a very low dose in a stable patient, and are contraindicated in acute decompensation.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Digoxin in practice`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Therapeutic plasma level 0.5-2 ng/mL; toxicity is common above 2 ng/mL, and there is no safe wide margin.`,`Eliminated mainly unchanged by the kidney, half-life about 36-40 hours, large volume of distribution (about 6-7 L/kg), so a loading dose is used.`,`Uses: atrial fibrillation and flutter with fast ventricular rate, and symptomatic systolic heart failure.`,`Ineffective or harmful in diastolic failure, hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy, Wolff-Parkinson-White with atrial fibrillation, and constrictive pericarditis.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Digoxin toxicity: system`,`Features`],rows:[[`Gastrointestinal (earliest)`,`Anorexia, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain`],[`Visual`,`Blurred vision, yellow-green halos (xanthopsia)`],[`CNS`,`Headache, fatigue, confusion, delirium`],[`Cardiac`,`Any arrhythmia; classically ventricular bigeminy and atrial tachycardia with AV block`]],caption:`Anorexia and nausea usually appear before the arrhythmia, which is why they must never be dismissed.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`What precipitates digoxin toxicity`,text:`HYPOkalaemia (thiazide and loop diuretics), hypomagnesaemia, HYPERcalcaemia, renal impairment, old age, hypothyroidism, and drugs that raise digoxin levels (quinidine, verapamil, amiodarone, erythromycin). Hypokalaemia is the single commonest cause because low potassium increases digoxin binding to the pump.`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Stop digoxin and any offending diuretic.`,`Correct potassium and magnesium (potassium is given unless there is AV block or hyperkalaemia).`,`For ventricular arrhythmia give lignocaine or phenytoin; for bradycardia or heart block give atropine and consider pacing.`,`Severe or life-threatening toxicity: digoxin-specific antibody Fab fragments (digibind).`,`Avoid DC cardioversion if possible — it can precipitate ventricular fibrillation in the digitalised heart.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Vaughan Williams classification`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Action`,`Effect on APD`,`Drugs`],rows:[[`IA`,`Moderate Na block plus K block`,`Prolonged`,`Quinidine, procainamide, disopyramide`],[`IB`,`Weak Na block, fast on-off`,`Shortened`,`Lignocaine, mexiletine, phenytoin`],[`IC`,`Marked Na block, slow on-off`,`Unchanged`,`Flecainide, propafenone`],[`II`,`Beta adrenergic block`,`Unchanged`,`Propranolol, metoprolol, esmolol`],[`III`,`Potassium channel block`,`Markedly prolonged`,`Amiodarone, sotalol, dofetilide, ibutilide, dronedarone`],[`IV`,`Calcium channel block (nodal)`,`Unchanged`,`Verapamil, diltiazem`],[`Unclassified`,`Various`,`-`,`Adenosine, digoxin, magnesium sulphate`]],caption:`APD = action potential duration. Note that lignocaine SHORTENS it while quinidine lengthens it, though both are class I.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Class I subclasses`,text:`Double Quarter Pounder for IA (Disopyramide, Quinidine, Procainamide); Lettuce Mayo Pickle for IB (Lignocaine, Mexiletine, Phenytoin); Fries Please for IC (Flecainide, Propafenone).`}]},{topicId:`ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced work here is the arrhythmia-specific drug of choice, amiodarone toxicity by organ, and the mechanistic exceptions that make examiners happy.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Arrhythmia`,`Drug of choice`,`Note`],rows:[[`Paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia`,`Adenosine IV bolus`,`Verapamil or vagal manoeuvres as alternatives`],[`Atrial fibrillation - rate control`,`Beta blocker or verapamil / diltiazem`,`Digoxin if heart failure or sedentary`],[`Atrial fibrillation - rhythm control`,`Amiodarone, flecainide, dronedarone`,`Flecainide only if no structural heart disease`],[`Ventricular tachycardia (stable)`,`Amiodarone IV; lignocaine as alternative`,`Unstable VT needs DC shock`],[`Digoxin-induced ventricular arrhythmia`,`Lignocaine or phenytoin`,`Avoid quinidine and DC shock`],[`Torsades de pointes`,`Magnesium sulphate IV`,`Stop the QT-prolonging drug, correct K and Mg`],[`WPW with atrial fibrillation`,`Procainamide or DC cardioversion`,`Digoxin, verapamil and adenosine are contraindicated`],[`Ventricular fibrillation`,`DC defibrillation, then amiodarone`,`Adrenaline per resuscitation protocol`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Why AV nodal blockers are banned in WPW with AF`,text:`Digoxin, verapamil and adenosine block the AV node, so atrial impulses divert down the accessory pathway. The ventricular rate can accelerate into ventricular fibrillation. Use a drug that blocks the accessory pathway itself, such as procainamide.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Amiodarone`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`It has all four Vaughan Williams actions: potassium block predominates, plus sodium block, beta block and calcium block.`,`Extremely long half-life of 25-60 days because of massive tissue accumulation, so loading is needed and effects persist for months after stopping.`,`Thyroid: it is 37 percent iodine by weight and causes both hypothyroidism and thyrotoxicosis. Check thyroid function before and 6-monthly.`,`Lung: pulmonary fibrosis and pneumonitis, the most feared toxicity.`,`Eye: corneal microdeposits in nearly everyone, usually asymptomatic; optic neuropathy is rare.`,`Skin: photosensitivity and slate-grey or bluish discolouration.`,`Liver: transaminase rise, rarely hepatitis. Also peripheral neuropathy and bradycardia.`,`It raises digoxin and warfarin levels, so both doses must be reduced.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Amiodarone versus sotalol`,text:`Both prolong QT, but amiodarone causes torsades de pointes far LESS often than expected because it prolongs repolarisation homogeneously across the myocardium. Sotalol shows reverse use-dependence and readily causes torsades. Dronedarone is the de-iodinated analogue without thyroid toxicity but is contraindicated in severe heart failure.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Adenosine acts on A1 receptors, opens acetylcholine-sensitive potassium channels and blocks the AV node; half-life is under 10 seconds so it must be given as a rapid bolus with a saline flush. Effect is antagonised by theophylline and potentiated by dipyridamole. It causes transient flushing, chest tightness and a sense of impending doom, and can cause bronchospasm in asthmatics.`,`Class IC agents (flecainide) increased mortality after myocardial infarction in the CAST trial and are contraindicated in structural heart disease.`,`Quinidine has an antimuscarinic action that can paradoxically speed AV conduction in atrial flutter, so an AV nodal blocker must be given first. It also causes cinchonism (tinnitus, headache, vertigo, blurred vision).`,`Procainamide causes drug-induced lupus, especially in slow acetylators; disopyramide has the strongest antimuscarinic and negative inotropic effects.`,`Lignocaine has high first-pass metabolism, so it is only given intravenously; it acts preferentially on ischaemic and depolarised tissue (use-dependent block).`,`Ivabradine blocks the If (funny) current in the SA node only, slowing rate without any effect on contractility or blood pressure.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`SGLT2 inhibitor benefit is independent of glycaemia`,caption:`Dapagliflozin and empagliflozin lower heart failure hospitalisation and cardiovascular death in patients with and without diabetes, through natriuresis, reduced preload and improved myocardial energetics.`}]},{topicId:`ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Cholesterol travels in the blood inside lipoproteins. Too much LDL deposits in artery walls and causes atherosclerosis. Hypolipidaemic drugs lower it. Separately, anticoagulants, antiplatelets and thrombolytics stop or dissolve clots that block those arteries.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Lipoprotein`,`Mainly carries`,`Role`],rows:[[`Chylomicron`,`Dietary triglyceride`,`Gut to tissues`],[`VLDL`,`Endogenous triglyceride`,`Liver to tissues`],[`LDL`,`Cholesterol`,`Delivers cholesterol to tissues - the bad one`],[`HDL`,`Cholesterol`,`Returns cholesterol to liver - the good one`]],caption:`Density rises as protein content rises and lipid content falls: chylomicron is the least dense, HDL the most.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Statins`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Statins (atorvastatin, rosuvastatin, simvastatin) block HMG-CoA reductase, the enzyme the liver uses to make cholesterol.`,`The liver responds by making more LDL receptors, which pull LDL out of the blood.`,`They are the most effective LDL-lowering drugs and the only class with the strongest survival evidence.`,`Main side effects: muscle ache (myalgia), rarely severe muscle breakdown (rhabdomyolysis), and a rise in liver enzymes.`,`They are contraindicated in pregnancy and in active liver disease.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Which drug for which lipid`,text:`Statin for high LDL. Fibrate for high triglyceride. Niacin raises HDL the most. Match the drug to the abnormal number.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Stopping clots`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Anticoagulants (heparin, warfarin) stop the clotting proteins and work best on clots in slow-moving venous blood.`,`Antiplatelets (aspirin, clopidogrel) stop platelets sticking together and work best in fast-flowing arteries.`,`Thrombolytics (streptokinase, alteplase) dissolve a clot that has already formed.`,`Heparin is given by injection and acts immediately. Warfarin is taken by mouth and takes 3-5 days to act fully.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Antidotes to memorise`,text:`Heparin is reversed by protamine sulphate. Warfarin is reversed by vitamin K (phytonadione) plus fresh frozen plasma or prothrombin complex concentrate when bleeding is severe. Dabigatran is reversed by idarucizumab.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Aspirin in low dose (75-150 mg daily) irreversibly blocks the platelet enzyme cyclo-oxygenase-1, so that platelet cannot make thromboxane A2 for the rest of its 7-10 day life. That is why one small daily dose is enough.`}]},{topicId:`ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The two big comparison tables of this topic are the hypolipidaemic classes with their effect on each lipid fraction, and heparin versus warfarin point by point.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Drugs`,`Mechanism`,`Main lipid effect`],rows:[[`Statins`,`Atorvastatin, rosuvastatin, simvastatin`,`HMG-CoA reductase inhibition, LDL receptor up-regulation`,`LDL down 20-55 percent`],[`Fibrates`,`Fenofibrate, gemfibrozil, bezafibrate`,`PPAR-alpha agonist, lipoprotein lipase up`,`TG down 30-50 percent, HDL up`],[`Ezetimibe`,`Ezetimibe`,`Blocks NPC1L1 sterol transporter in jejunum`,`LDL down 15-20 percent`],[`Bile acid sequestrants`,`Cholestyramine, colestipol, colesevelam`,`Bind bile acids in gut, force new synthesis`,`LDL down 15-25 percent, TG may RISE`],[`Niacin (nicotinic acid)`,`Niacin`,`Inhibits hormone-sensitive lipase, lowers VLDL`,`Greatest HDL rise; lowers Lp(a)`],[`PCSK9 inhibitors`,`Evolocumab, alirocumab, inclisiran`,`Prevent LDL receptor degradation`,`LDL down 50-60 percent`],[`Omega-3 fatty acids`,`Icosapent ethyl`,`Reduced hepatic VLDL secretion`,`TG down`]],caption:`Only statins, ezetimibe and PCSK9 inhibitors have clear outcome benefit for LDL lowering.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Statin adverse effects and interactions`,text:`Myopathy risk rises with fibrates (especially gemfibrozil), macrolides, azole antifungals, ciclosporin, verapamil and grapefruit juice, all of which inhibit CYP3A4. Pravastatin, rosuvastatin and pitavastatin are least CYP3A4 dependent. Check creatine kinase if muscle pain occurs; new-onset diabetes is a recognised class effect.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Niacin causes intense cutaneous flushing mediated by prostaglandin D2; it is reduced by taking aspirin 30 minutes beforehand. It also causes hyperuricaemia, hyperglycaemia and hepatotoxicity (worst with sustained-release forms).`,`Bile acid sequestrants are not absorbed, so their side effects are all luminal: constipation, bloating and interference with the absorption of warfarin, digoxin, thiazides and fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K. Give other drugs 1 hour before or 4 hours after.`,`Fibrates cause myopathy, gallstones (raised biliary cholesterol saturation) and a rise in creatinine; fenofibrate is preferred over gemfibrozil in combination with a statin.`,`Type I hyperlipoproteinaemia (chylomicronaemia from lipoprotein lipase deficiency) does not respond to statins; treatment is dietary fat restriction.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Heparin versus warfarin`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Heparin`,`Warfarin`],rows:[[`Source / nature`,`Mucopolysaccharide, strongly acidic`,`Synthetic coumarin`],[`Route`,`IV or subcutaneous only`,`Oral`],[`Onset`,`Immediate (IV)`,`3-5 days (delayed)`],[`Site of action`,`In vivo and in vitro`,`In vivo only`],[`Mechanism`,`Activates antithrombin III, inhibits IIa and Xa`,`Inhibits vitamin K epoxide reductase, so factors II, VII, IX, X and proteins C and S are not carboxylated`],[`Monitoring`,`aPTT (1.5-2.5 x control)`,`INR (target 2-3, or 2.5-3.5 with mechanical valve)`],[`Antidote`,`Protamine sulphate`,`Vitamin K, FFP, PCC`],[`Use in pregnancy`,`Safe (does not cross placenta)`,`Contraindicated - teratogenic`],[`Duration after stopping`,`Hours`,`Days`],[`Main hazard`,`Bleeding, HIT, osteoporosis, hyperkalaemia`,`Bleeding, skin necrosis, teratogenicity`]],caption:`The single most examined table in cardiovascular pharmacology. Learn every row.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Why heparin overlaps with warfarin`,text:`Warfarin first depletes protein C, whose half-life (about 8 hours) is shorter than that of prothrombin (about 60 hours), producing a brief PROcoagulant state that can cause skin necrosis. Heparin must therefore be continued for at least 5 days and until the INR has been in range for 2 days.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Antiplatelet drugs`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug`,`Target`,`Nature of block`],rows:[[`Aspirin`,`COX-1, thromboxane A2 synthesis`,`Irreversible, lasts platelet lifetime`],[`Clopidogrel, prasugrel`,`P2Y12 ADP receptor`,`Irreversible; both are prodrugs`],[`Ticagrelor, cangrelor`,`P2Y12 ADP receptor`,`Reversible, not a prodrug, faster onset`],[`Abciximab, eptifibatide, tirofiban`,`Glycoprotein IIb/IIIa (final common pathway)`,`IV only, used in PCI`],[`Dipyridamole, cilostazol`,`Phosphodiesterase, adenosine uptake`,`Reversible; cilostazol for claudication`]]}]},{topicId:`ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The advanced band covers LMWH and DOAC selection, heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, thrombolytic differences and the numerical detail of warfarin interactions.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Unfractionated heparin`,`LMWH (enoxaparin, dalteparin)`,`Fondaparinux`],rows:[[`Molecular weight`,`5000-30000 Da`,`2000-6000 Da`,`Pentasaccharide, about 1728 Da`],[`Target`,`Xa and IIa equally`,`Mainly Xa (anti-Xa:IIa about 3:1)`,`Xa only`],[`Bioavailability SC`,`About 30 percent, variable`,`About 90 percent, predictable`,`About 100 percent`],[`Monitoring`,`aPTT needed`,`Usually none (anti-Xa if renal failure, pregnancy, obesity)`,`None`],[`Risk of HIT`,`Highest`,`Lower`,`Negligible`],[`Protamine reversal`,`Complete`,`Partial (about 60 percent)`,`Not reversed`]],caption:`A drug must bridge both antithrombin III and thrombin (at least 18 saccharide units) to inhibit IIa. Short chains can only inhibit Xa.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT)`,text:`Type II HIT is immune, appears 5-10 days after starting heparin, drops the platelet count by more than 50 percent and paradoxically causes THROMBOSIS, not bleeding. Antibodies target the heparin-platelet factor 4 complex. Stop all heparin including flushes and LMWH, and switch to a direct thrombin inhibitor such as argatroban or bivalirudin. Warfarin must not be started until platelets recover.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`DOAC`,`Target`,`Antidote`,`Note`],rows:[[`Dabigatran`,`Direct thrombin (IIa)`,`Idarucizumab`,`Prodrug; largely renal, dialysable`],[`Rivaroxaban`,`Direct factor Xa`,`Andexanet alfa`,`Once daily with food`],[`Apixaban`,`Direct factor Xa`,`Andexanet alfa`,`Least renal dependence`],[`Edoxaban`,`Direct factor Xa`,`Andexanet alfa`,`Once daily`]],caption:`DOACs need no routine monitoring and have fewer food and drug interactions, but they are contraindicated in mechanical heart valves and severe renal impairment, where warfarin remains the drug of choice.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Warfarin is 99 percent protein bound and metabolised by CYP2C9; the S-enantiomer is 3-5 times more potent than the R-enantiomer.`,`INR RISES with amiodarone, metronidazole, cotrimoxazole, fluconazole, ciprofloxacin, cimetidine, aspirin and NSAIDs.`,`INR FALLS with rifampicin, carbamazepine, phenytoin, phenobarbitone, griseofulvin, oral contraceptives and a vitamin K rich diet (green leafy vegetables).`,`Management of high INR without bleeding: INR 5-9 means withhold warfarin, consider oral vitamin K 1-2.5 mg. With serious bleeding give IV vitamin K plus prothrombin complex concentrate, since vitamin K alone takes 6-24 hours.`,`Warfarin embryopathy in the first trimester gives nasal hypoplasia and stippled epiphyses; late pregnancy use risks fetal intracranial haemorrhage.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Thrombolytic`,`Fibrin selectivity`,`Antigenic?`,`Note`],rows:[[`Streptokinase`,`Non-selective`,`Yes - allergy, hypotension`,`From streptococci; cannot repeat within 1 year`],[`Urokinase`,`Non-selective`,`No`,`From human urine or kidney culture`],[`Alteplase (rt-PA)`,`Selective`,`No`,`Short half-life, needs infusion; used in stroke`],[`Reteplase`,`Selective`,`No`,`Double bolus`],[`Tenecteplase`,`Most selective`,`No`,`Single bolus, most convenient`]],caption:`Thrombolytics act by converting plasminogen to plasmin. Their antidotes are tranexamic acid and epsilon-aminocaproic acid.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Aspirin resistance and dosing`,text:`Higher aspirin doses inhibit endothelial COX-2 and therefore prostacyclin as well, which is antithrombotically counterproductive. That is why the antiplatelet dose stays at 75-150 mg daily, while analgesic doses are 10-50 times higher.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Clopidogrel needs CYP2C19 activation, so poor metabolisers and patients on omeprazole get less benefit; pantoprazole is preferred if a PPI is needed.`,`Ticagrelor causes dyspnoea and a rise in uric acid and creatinine; prasugrel is contraindicated after stroke or TIA and in patients over 75 kg under 60 kg or over 75 years without dose reduction.`,`Abciximab is a monoclonal Fab fragment with a long platelet-bound action; eptifibatide and tirofiban are small molecules with short action.`,`Vitamin K (phytonadione) is required for gamma-carboxylation of glutamate residues on factors II, VII, IX and X. Newborns are given 1 mg IM at birth to prevent haemorrhagic disease of the newborn.`,`Factor VII has the shortest half-life (4-6 hours), so the prothrombin time (INR) rises first when warfarin is started, before full anticoagulation is achieved.`]}]}],t=[{id:`q-ph-general-principles-1`,topicId:`ph-general-principles`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which of the following drugs acts through an intracellular (nuclear) receptor?`,options:[`Nicotine`,`Insulin`,`Propranolol`,`Dexamethasone`],answer:3,explanation:`Dexamethasone is lipid soluble, crosses the membrane and binds the cytosolic glucocorticoid receptor, which then alters gene transcription — hence the delay of hours. Insulin is the tempting distractor, but its receptor is a transmembrane tyrosine kinase, not a nuclear receptor.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-general-principles-2`,topicId:`ph-general-principles`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The potency of a drug is best represented on a graded dose-response curve by its:`,options:[`Maximal effect (Emax)`,`ED50`,`Slope of the curve`,`Intrinsic activity`],answer:1,explanation:`Potency is a dose axis property and is read as the ED50 or EC50; the more potent drug has the curve further to the left. Emax is the wrong choice because it measures efficacy, which is an effect axis property and is entirely independent of potency.`},{id:`q-ph-general-principles-3`,topicId:`ph-general-principles`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Adding a competitive antagonist to an agonist dose-response curve produces:`,options:[`A parallel shift to the right with Emax unchanged`,`A downward shift with Emax reduced`,`A parallel shift to the left with Emax unchanged`,`No change in the curve until receptors are saturated`],answer:0,explanation:`A competitive antagonist occupies the same site reversibly, so more agonist restores the full maximum — the curve moves right in parallel and Emax is preserved. A downward shift with Emax reduced describes non-competitive or irreversible antagonism, where the maximum can never be regained however much agonist is added.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-general-principles-4`,topicId:`ph-general-principles`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A drug has an ED50 of 5 mg/kg and an LD50 of 250 mg/kg. Its therapeutic index is:`,options:[`50`,`25`,`125`,`1250`],answer:0,explanation:`TI = LD50 / ED50 = 250 / 5 = 50. The distractor 1250 comes from multiplying rather than dividing, and 125 from halving the LD50 first. Note that a large TI indicates safety, not potency.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-ph-general-principles-5`,topicId:`ph-general-principles`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Rapidly developing loss of response after only a few repeated doses of ephedrine is called:`,options:[`Tachyphylaxis`,`Idiosyncrasy`,`Tolerance`,`Drug resistance`],answer:0,explanation:`Ephedrine acts indirectly by releasing stored noradrenaline; the stores empty within minutes to hours, so the response fades acutely — that is tachyphylaxis. Tolerance is the wrong answer because it develops over days to weeks and involves receptor down-regulation or enzyme induction.`},{id:`q-ph-general-principles-6`,topicId:`ph-general-principles`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Adrenaline relieving histamine-induced bronchospasm is an example of:`,options:[`Competitive antagonism`,`Chemical antagonism`,`Non-competitive antagonism`,`Physiological (functional) antagonism`],answer:3,explanation:`Adrenaline acts on beta-2 receptors while histamine acts on H1 receptors; two different receptors producing opposite effects on the same tissue is physiological antagonism. Competitive antagonism is wrong because the two drugs never compete for the same binding site — that would describe chlorpheniramine.`},{id:`q-ph-general-principles-7`,topicId:`ph-general-principles`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Failure of an oral contraceptive in a patient started on rifampicin is classified as which type of adverse drug reaction?`,options:[`Type A (augmented)`,`Type B (bizarre)`,`Type F (failure of therapy)`,`Type E (end of use)`],answer:2,explanation:`Type F is unexpected failure of therapy, commonly from an interaction — here rifampicin induces CYP3A4 and accelerates oestrogen metabolism. Type E is the trap: it means a withdrawal reaction on stopping a drug, such as rebound hypertension after clonidine, not loss of effect during therapy.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-general-principles-8`,topicId:`ph-general-principles`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In the presence of a competitive antagonist the agonist EC50 shifts from 2 nM to 8 nM. The dose ratio and the nature of the Schild plot slope confirming simple competition are:`,options:[`Dose ratio 4, slope 1`,`Dose ratio 3, slope 1`,`Dose ratio 4, slope 2`,`Dose ratio 6, slope 0.5`],answer:0,explanation:`Dose ratio = EC50 with antagonist / EC50 without = 8 / 2 = 4. A Schild plot of log(dose ratio - 1) against log[antagonist] must have a slope of unity for simple competitive antagonism; a dose ratio of 3 is the tempting error from using (dose ratio - 1) as the ratio itself.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-ph-ans-cholinergic-1`,topicId:`ph-ans-cholinergic`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Stimulation of cardiac M2 receptors by acetylcholine produces:`,options:[`Tachycardia`,`Increased force of contraction`,`Mydriasis`,`Bradycardia and slowed AV conduction`],answer:3,explanation:`M2 receptors are Gi coupled; they lower cAMP and open potassium channels in nodal tissue, slowing the rate and AV conduction. Mydriasis is the opposite of the cholinergic effect on the eye, which is miosis through the M3-mediated sphincter pupillae.`},{id:`q-ph-ans-cholinergic-2`,topicId:`ph-ans-cholinergic`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A patient with Datura (atropine) poisoning has delirium, dry hot skin and dilated pupils. The anticholinesterase of choice is:`,options:[`Neostigmine`,`Edrophonium`,`Pyridostigmine`,`Physostigmine`],answer:3,explanation:`Physostigmine is a tertiary amine that crosses the blood-brain barrier and reverses the central antimuscarinic syndrome. Neostigmine is the classic distractor, but its quaternary nitrogen keeps it out of the CNS so it cannot treat the delirium.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-ans-cholinergic-3`,topicId:`ph-ans-cholinergic`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Pirenzepine reduces gastric acid secretion by blocking which receptor?`,options:[`M1`,`M2`,`M3`,`Nn`],answer:0,explanation:`Pirenzepine is a selective M1 antagonist and M1 receptors mediate the cholinergic drive to gastric acid secretion via enterochromaffin-like cells and ganglia. M3 is tempting because it is the smooth muscle and glandular subtype, but the acid-secretory selectivity of pirenzepine is specifically M1.`},{id:`q-ph-ans-cholinergic-4`,topicId:`ph-ans-cholinergic`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In organophosphate poisoning, which feature is relieved by pralidoxime but NOT by atropine?`,options:[`Bronchial secretions`,`Miosis`,`Muscle fasciculation and weakness`,`Bradycardia`],answer:2,explanation:`Fasciculation and respiratory muscle weakness are nicotinic effects at the skeletal end plate, where atropine has no action at all; only reactivation of the enzyme by pralidoxime helps. Secretions, miosis and bradycardia are all muscarinic and are controlled by atropine.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-ans-cholinergic-5`,topicId:`ph-ans-cholinergic`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which antimuscarinic is preferred as a preanaesthetic drying agent when central effects must be avoided?`,options:[`Atropine`,`Hyoscine`,`Glycopyrrolate`,`Tropicamide`],answer:2,explanation:`Glycopyrrolate is a quaternary ammonium compound, so it does not cross the blood-brain barrier and dries secretions without sedation or delirium. Hyoscine is the trap: it is the most centrally active of the group and causes sedation and amnesia.`},{id:`q-ph-ans-cholinergic-6`,topicId:`ph-ans-cholinergic`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Bethanechol differs from acetylcholine in that it is:`,options:[`Resistant to cholinesterase and muscarinic selective`,`A nicotinic selective agonist`,`An indirectly acting cholinomimetic`,`Rapidly hydrolysed but longer acting on the heart`],answer:0,explanation:`The beta-methyl group makes bethanechol resistant to acetylcholinesterase and confers muscarinic selectivity with negligible nicotinic action, which is why it is safe orally for urinary retention. Carbachol, by contrast, is resistant to hydrolysis but retains substantial nicotinic activity.`},{id:`q-ph-ans-cholinergic-7`,topicId:`ph-ans-cholinergic`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Fade on tetanic stimulation and post-tetanic potentiation during neuromuscular monitoring indicate:`,options:[`Phase I depolarising block`,`Local anaesthetic effect at the end plate`,`Myasthenic crisis`,`Non-depolarising competitive block`],answer:3,explanation:`Both fade and post-tetanic potentiation are hallmarks of competitive (non-depolarising) blockade, and such a block is reversed by neostigmine. Phase I succinylcholine block shows neither fade nor post-tetanic potentiation, and is actually deepened by neostigmine.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-ans-cholinergic-8`,topicId:`ph-ans-cholinergic`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A patient in renal and hepatic failure needs neuromuscular blockade. The most appropriate agent is:`,options:[`Pancuronium`,`Atracurium`,`Succinylcholine`,`d-Tubocurarine`],answer:1,explanation:`Atracurium undergoes spontaneous Hofmann elimination and ester hydrolysis in plasma, so its clearance is independent of renal and hepatic function. Pancuronium is the trap because it is largely excreted unchanged by the kidney and would accumulate dangerously.`},{id:`q-ph-ans-adrenergic-1`,topicId:`ph-ans-adrenergic`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The rate-limiting enzyme in catecholamine biosynthesis is:`,options:[`DOPA decarboxylase`,`Phenylethanolamine-N-methyltransferase`,`Dopamine beta-hydroxylase`,`Tyrosine hydroxylase`],answer:3,explanation:`Tyrosine hydroxylase converts tyrosine to DOPA and is the slowest, rate-limiting and feedback-regulated step, inhibited by metirosine. PNMT is the tempting choice but it acts only in the adrenal medulla and only on the final conversion of noradrenaline to adrenaline.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-ans-adrenergic-2`,topicId:`ph-ans-adrenergic`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Bronchodilation in asthma is mediated through which receptor?`,options:[`Alpha-1`,`Beta-1`,`Beta-2`,`Alpha-2`],answer:2,explanation:`Beta-2 receptors on bronchial smooth muscle are Gs coupled; raising cAMP relaxes the airway, which is why salbutamol is a beta-2 agonist. Beta-1 is the cardiac subtype and stimulating it would give tachycardia without relieving bronchospasm.`},{id:`q-ph-ans-adrenergic-3`,topicId:`ph-ans-adrenergic`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`At an infusion rate of 1-2 micrograms/kg/min, dopamine acts predominantly on:`,options:[`D1 receptors in renal vessels`,`Beta-1 receptors`,`Alpha-1 receptors`,`Beta-2 receptors`],answer:0,explanation:`The lowest dose range activates D1 receptors and dilates renal and mesenteric vessels, promoting natriuresis. Beta-1 inotropy is recruited at 2-10 micrograms/kg/min and alpha-1 vasoconstriction only above 10, at which point the renal benefit is lost.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-ans-adrenergic-4`,topicId:`ph-ans-adrenergic`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`After pretreatment with phentolamine, adrenaline causes a FALL in blood pressure. This phenomenon is:`,options:[`Tachyphylaxis`,`Adrenaline reversal`,`Physiological antagonism`,`Denervation supersensitivity`],answer:1,explanation:`Blocking alpha-1 receptors leaves the beta-2 mediated vasodilator action of adrenaline unopposed, so the pressor response is converted to a depressor one — adrenaline reversal or the Dale vinculum. Noradrenaline is never reversed because it has almost no beta-2 activity.`},{id:`q-ph-ans-adrenergic-5`,topicId:`ph-ans-adrenergic`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which beta blocker possesses intrinsic sympathomimetic activity (ISA)?`,options:[`Propranolol`,`Atenolol`,`Pindolol`,`Timolol`],answer:2,explanation:`Pindolol (with acebutolol and celiprolol) is a partial agonist, so it causes less resting bradycardia and less disturbance of lipid profile. Propranolol is the standard non-selective blocker with no ISA but high membrane stabilising activity, which is a different property altogether.`},{id:`q-ph-ans-adrenergic-6`,topicId:`ph-ans-adrenergic`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A beta blocker is best avoided in which of the following?`,options:[`Post-myocardial infarction`,`Thyrotoxicosis`,`Variant (Prinzmetal) angina`,`Migraine prophylaxis`],answer:2,explanation:`Variant angina is caused by coronary spasm; blocking beta-2 mediated coronary dilation leaves alpha-1 vasoconstriction unopposed and can worsen the spasm. Beta blockers are actively indicated in the other three situations, particularly after myocardial infarction where they reduce mortality.`},{id:`q-ph-ans-adrenergic-7`,topicId:`ph-ans-adrenergic`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In a patient pretreated with cocaine, the pressor response to tyramine is:`,options:[`Markedly potentiated`,`Unchanged`,`Reduced or abolished`,`Converted to a fall in blood pressure`],answer:2,explanation:`Tyramine is an indirectly acting amine that must enter the nerve terminal through uptake-1 to displace stored noradrenaline; cocaine blocks uptake-1 and therefore denies it entry. The trap is to apply the general rule that cocaine potentiates catecholamines — that holds only for directly acting agents such as noradrenaline.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-ans-adrenergic-8`,topicId:`ph-ans-adrenergic`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In preparing a patient with phaeochromocytoma for surgery, the correct sequence is:`,options:[`Beta blocker first, then alpha blocker`,`Calcium channel blocker followed by an ACE inhibitor`,`Beta blocker alone is sufficient`,`Alpha blocker (phenoxybenzamine) first, then beta blocker`],answer:3,explanation:`Alpha blockade with phenoxybenzamine must come first so that vasoconstriction is controlled; a beta blocker is then added for reflex tachycardia. Starting with the beta blocker removes beta-2 vasodilation and leaves alpha-mediated vasoconstriction unopposed, which can precipitate a hypertensive crisis.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics-1`,topicId:`ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Benzodiazepines act on the GABA-A receptor by:`,options:[`Increasing the frequency of chloride channel opening`,`Increasing the duration of chloride channel opening`,`Directly opening the chloride channel without GABA`,`Blocking GABA reuptake`],answer:0,explanation:`Benzodiazepines are positive allosteric modulators that raise the frequency of channel opening in the presence of GABA. Increasing the duration of opening is the barbiturate action, and opening the channel directly without GABA is what barbiturates do at high dose — which is exactly why barbiturates have no ceiling effect and are lethal in overdose while benzodiazepines largely are not.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics-2`,topicId:`ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The drug of choice for uncomplicated absence (petit mal) seizures is:`,options:[`Carbamazepine`,`Phenytoin`,`Ethosuximide`,`Gabapentin`],answer:2,explanation:`Ethosuximide blocks T-type calcium currents in thalamic neurones, the exact pacemaker of the 3 Hz spike-and-wave discharge. Carbamazepine is not merely ineffective here — along with phenytoin and gabapentin it can aggravate absence and myoclonic seizures.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics-3`,topicId:`ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Levetiracetam produces its antiepileptic effect by binding:`,options:[`Voltage-gated sodium channels`,`Synaptic vesicle protein SV2A`,`The GABA-A benzodiazepine site`,`T-type calcium channels`],answer:1,explanation:`Levetiracetam binds SV2A and modulates vesicular neurotransmitter release, a mechanism unique to it and brivaracetam. Sodium channel block is the mechanism of phenytoin, carbamazepine and lamotrigine, none of which share the interaction-free profile of levetiracetam.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics-4`,topicId:`ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Flumazenil is of no value in which of the following?`,options:[`Zolpidem overdose`,`Diazepam overdose`,`Phenobarbitone overdose`,`Reversal of midazolam sedation after endoscopy`],answer:2,explanation:`Flumazenil is a competitive antagonist at the benzodiazepine site only, so it reverses benzodiazepines and Z-drugs but not barbiturates, which bind a separate site. Barbiturate overdose has no antidote and is managed by supportive care and alkaline diuresis.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics-5`,topicId:`ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A patient on phenytoin has a stable level of 15 mcg/mL. A small dose increase produces a level of 32 mcg/mL with nystagmus and ataxia. This is because phenytoin shows:`,options:[`First-order kinetics with a short half-life`,`Autoinduction of its own metabolism`,`Enterohepatic recycling`,`Saturation (zero-order) kinetics within the therapeutic range`],answer:3,explanation:`CYP2C9 and CYP2C19 saturate at therapeutic phenytoin concentrations, so elimination becomes zero order and a small dose rise gives a disproportionate level rise. Autoinduction is the distractor drawn from carbamazepine, which lowers rather than raises its own levels over the first weeks.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics-6`,topicId:`ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The first-line intravenous drug in status epilepticus is:`,options:[`Lorazepam`,`Phenobarbitone`,`Valproate`,`Thiopentone`],answer:0,explanation:`IV lorazepam is preferred over diazepam because it redistributes less from brain to fat and so has a longer anticonvulsant duration despite a similar onset. Thiopentone and phenobarbitone belong to the refractory stage, after benzodiazepines and a second-line agent such as fosphenytoin have failed.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics-7`,topicId:`ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`When lamotrigine is added to sodium valproate, the lamotrigine dose must be reduced because valproate:`,options:[`Induces the CYP3A4 metabolism of lamotrigine`,`Inhibits the glucuronidation of lamotrigine`,`Displaces lamotrigine from plasma proteins`,`Reduces the renal clearance of lamotrigine`],answer:1,explanation:`Valproate is an enzyme inhibitor that blocks UGT-mediated glucuronidation, roughly doubling lamotrigine levels and sharply raising the risk of Stevens-Johnson syndrome. Induction is the trap because every other major antiepileptic (phenytoin, carbamazepine, phenobarbitone) does induce and therefore lowers lamotrigine.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics-8`,topicId:`ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A patient on an antiepileptic develops hyponatraemia and diplopia, and genetic testing shows HLA-B*1502. The drug is most likely:`,options:[`Carbamazepine`,`Levetiracetam`,`Valproate`,`Topiramate`],answer:0,explanation:`Carbamazepine causes SIADH-type hyponatraemia and diplopia, and HLA-B*1502 in patients of Asian ancestry predicts carbamazepine-induced Stevens-Johnson syndrome. Valproate is the distractor for hepatotoxicity, weight gain and neural tube defects, but it does not cause hyponatraemia.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants-1`,topicId:`ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The antipsychotic benefit of D2 blockade is exerted mainly on which dopamine pathway?`,options:[`Nigrostriatal`,`Mesolimbic`,`Tuberoinfundibular`,`Mesocortical`],answer:1,explanation:`Blockade of the mesolimbic pathway abolishes hallucinations and delusions, the positive symptoms. The nigrostriatal pathway is the distractor: blocking it produces the extrapyramidal side effects rather than any therapeutic benefit.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants-2`,topicId:`ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which antipsychotic requires regular white blood cell counts?`,options:[`Haloperidol`,`Risperidone`,`Clozapine`,`Chlorpromazine`],answer:2,explanation:`Clozapine causes agranulocytosis in 1-2 percent of patients, so counts are checked weekly at first and then monthly. Chlorpromazine can rarely cause blood dyscrasias but does not carry a mandatory monitoring requirement, and its typical problems are jaundice and photosensitivity.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants-3`,topicId:`ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which extrapyramidal reaction is WORSENED by anticholinergic drugs?`,options:[`Acute dystonia`,`Akathisia`,`Drug-induced parkinsonism`,`Tardive dyskinesia`],answer:3,explanation:`Tardive dyskinesia reflects dopamine receptor supersensitivity, so reducing cholinergic tone tips the balance further towards dopamine and aggravates the movements. Acute dystonia is the opposite case — it responds within minutes to parenteral benztropine or promethazine.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants-4`,topicId:`ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A patient on haloperidol develops fever, lead-pipe rigidity, autonomic instability and a creatine kinase of 4000 U/L. The specific treatment is:`,options:[`Cyproheptadine`,`Sodium bicarbonate`,`Physostigmine`,`Dantrolene with bromocriptine`],answer:3,explanation:`This is neuroleptic malignant syndrome, treated by stopping the antipsychotic and giving dantrolene for the rigidity plus a dopamine agonist such as bromocriptine or amantadine. Cyproheptadine is the trap because it treats serotonin syndrome, which presents with clonus, hyperreflexia and dilated pupils instead of lead-pipe rigidity.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants-5`,topicId:`ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which antidepressant causes marked sedation and weight gain but is notably free of sexual dysfunction?`,options:[`Fluoxetine`,`Mirtazapine`,`Venlafaxine`,`Imipramine`],answer:1,explanation:`Mirtazapine blocks presynaptic alpha-2 receptors and also 5-HT2 and 5-HT3, so it raises noradrenaline and serotonin without the 5-HT2 mediated sexual dysfunction, while H1 blockade gives sedation and appetite. Fluoxetine is the distractor, but SSRIs are the class most associated with sexual dysfunction.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants-6`,topicId:`ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Aripiprazole differs from other atypical antipsychotics because it is a:`,options:[`Pure D2 antagonist with high potency`,`Muscarinic antagonist`,`Selective 5-HT2A antagonist without D2 action`,`D2 partial agonist`],answer:3,explanation:`Aripiprazole is a D2 partial agonist, stabilising dopamine transmission — it dampens excess mesolimbic activity while supporting nigrostriatal and tuberoinfundibular function, giving low EPS and low prolactin. A pure high-potency antagonist describes haloperidol, which has the opposite profile.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants-7`,topicId:`ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A patient stable on lithium is started on a thiazide diuretic and develops coarse tremor, vomiting and ataxia. The mechanism is:`,options:[`Increased proximal tubular reabsorption of lithium following sodium depletion`,`Inhibition of hepatic lithium metabolism`,`Displacement of lithium from plasma proteins`,`Reduced glomerular filtration caused by the thiazide`],answer:0,explanation:`Thiazides deplete sodium, so the proximal tubule compensates by reabsorbing more sodium and, because lithium is handled like sodium, more lithium as well, and the level rises into the toxic range. Protein displacement is impossible here since lithium is an ion and is not protein bound at all.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants-8`,topicId:`ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A patient on phenelzine eats aged cheese and develops a severe throbbing headache with blood pressure 230/130 mmHg. The drug of choice is:`,options:[`Phentolamine`,`Propranolol`,`Atropine`,`Cyproheptadine`],answer:0,explanation:`The cheese reaction is a hypertensive crisis caused by tyramine-released noradrenaline acting on alpha-1 receptors, so an alpha blocker such as phentolamine is the specific treatment. Propranolol would leave alpha-mediated vasoconstriction unopposed and can make the pressure worse.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics-1`,topicId:`ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The classic triad of acute opioid overdose is:`,options:[`Mydriasis, tachycardia and hyperthermia`,`Pinpoint pupils, respiratory depression and coma`,`Rigidity, hyperthermia and raised creatine kinase`,`Convulsions, hypertension and sweating`],answer:1,explanation:`Miosis, depressed respiration and coma together are almost diagnostic of opioid poisoning, and naloxone reverses them within minutes. Mydriasis with tachycardia and hyperthermia is the anticholinergic picture, the opposite syndrome.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics-2`,topicId:`ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Levodopa is combined with carbidopa in order to:`,options:[`Increase its absorption from the stomach`,`Prevent the on-off phenomenon`,`Inhibit central COMT`,`Inhibit peripheral DOPA decarboxylase and increase brain delivery`],answer:3,explanation:`Carbidopa does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so it blocks decarboxylation only in the periphery, cutting the levodopa dose by about 75 percent and reducing nausea and hypotension. COMT inhibition is the action of entacapone and tolcapone, a different adjunct entirely.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics-3`,topicId:`ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A low blood-gas partition coefficient in an inhalational anaesthetic indicates:`,options:[`High potency`,`Rapid induction and recovery`,`Low MAC`,`High lipid solubility`],answer:1,explanation:`Low blood solubility means the alveolar partial pressure equilibrates quickly with the brain, so induction and recovery are fast — this is why desflurane and nitrous oxide act quickly. Potency is a separate property determined by the oil-gas coefficient and expressed as MAC.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics-4`,topicId:`ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Tolerance on chronic morphine therapy does NOT develop to which effect?`,options:[`Analgesia`,`Euphoria`,`Constipation`,`Respiratory depression`],answer:2,explanation:`Constipation and miosis persist unchanged throughout long-term opioid therapy, which is why every patient on chronic opioids needs a laxative. Tolerance does develop to analgesia, euphoria, sedation and respiratory depression, which is why doses escalate.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics-5`,topicId:`ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Local anaesthetics are less effective in inflamed tissue because:`,options:[`Inflammation destroys sodium channels`,`Inflammation causes vasoconstriction and reduces delivery`,`The drug is metabolised faster in inflamed tissue`,`Acidic pH increases the ionised fraction, reducing membrane penetration`],answer:3,explanation:`Local anaesthetics are weak bases with pKa around 8; the acidic pH of inflamed tissue shifts the equilibrium towards the ionised form, and only the unionised species can cross the axon membrane. Vasoconstriction is wrong because inflamed tissue is vasodilated, which actually removes the drug faster.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics-6`,topicId:`ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The action of a single intravenous dose of thiopentone is terminated mainly by:`,options:[`Hepatic metabolism`,`Renal excretion`,`Redistribution to muscle and fat`,`Plasma cholinesterase hydrolysis`],answer:2,explanation:`Thiopentone is highly lipid soluble and enters the brain within seconds, then redistributes into muscle and fat, so consciousness returns in 5-10 minutes although the drug is still in the body. Hepatic metabolism is slow and only removes the drug over many hours, which is why repeated doses accumulate.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics-7`,topicId:`ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Adrenaline should NOT be added to a local anaesthetic for a ring block of a finger because:`,options:[`It shortens the duration of the block`,`It increases systemic toxicity of the anaesthetic`,`Digits are supplied by end arteries and vasoconstriction risks gangrene`,`It causes methaemoglobinaemia`],answer:2,explanation:`Fingers, toes, nose, ear lobes and penis are supplied by end arteries with no collateral flow, so adrenaline-induced vasoconstriction can produce ischaemic necrosis. Adrenaline actually prolongs the block and reduces systemic toxicity, so the first two options state the opposite of the truth.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics-8`,topicId:`ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Malignant hyperthermia triggered by succinylcholine and halothane is treated with:`,options:[`Bromocriptine`,`Dantrolene`,`Neostigmine`,`Naloxone`],answer:1,explanation:`Dantrolene blocks the defective ryanodine receptor and halts the uncontrolled release of calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, together with cooling and withdrawal of the trigger. Bromocriptine is the distractor from neuroleptic malignant syndrome, where a dopamine agonist is needed because the primary lesion is central dopamine blockade.`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-antihypertensives-1`,topicId:`ph-cvs-antihypertensives`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Dry cough with an ACE inhibitor is caused by accumulation of:`,options:[`Angiotensin II`,`Bradykinin and substance P`,`Aldosterone`,`Renin`],answer:1,explanation:`ACE is also kininase II, so inhibiting it prevents the breakdown of bradykinin and substance P in the airway, provoking cough and rarely angioedema. Angiotensin II is the distractor but its level FALLS, which is the therapeutic effect, not the cause of cough.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-antihypertensives-2`,topicId:`ph-cvs-antihypertensives`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Glyceryl trinitrate is given sublingually for an acute anginal attack chiefly because:`,options:[`It is destroyed by gastric acid`,`The sublingual route avoids reflex tachycardia`,`It is not absorbed from the intestine`,`It has an extensive hepatic first-pass metabolism`],answer:3,explanation:`GTN undergoes near-complete first-pass metabolism by hepatic organic nitrate reductase, so an oral dose would leave almost nothing in the circulation; the sublingual route drains into the superior vena cava and bypasses the liver. Isosorbide mononitrate, its active metabolite, escapes this problem and is fully bioavailable orally.`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-antihypertensives-3`,topicId:`ph-cvs-antihypertensives`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The antihypertensive of choice in a pregnant woman with chronic hypertension is:`,options:[`Enalapril`,`Losartan`,`Methyldopa or labetalol`,`Spironolactone`],answer:2,explanation:`Methyldopa has the longest safety record in pregnancy and labetalol is the usual first choice today; nifedipine is the accepted third option. ACE inhibitors and ARBs are absolutely contraindicated because they cause fetal renal failure, oligohydramnios and skull hypoplasia.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-antihypertensives-4`,topicId:`ph-cvs-antihypertensives`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The principal antianginal action of nitrates is:`,options:[`Dilation of the coronary arteries to increase flow`,`Reduction of preload by venodilation, lowering oxygen demand`,`Reduction of heart rate through vagal stimulation`,`Direct negative inotropic action on the myocardium`],answer:1,explanation:`At therapeutic doses nitrates dilate capacitance veins far more than arteries, so preload, ventricular wall tension and oxygen demand all fall. Coronary dilation is the classic distractor, but in fixed atheromatous stenosis it contributes little and can even cause steal.`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-antihypertensives-5`,topicId:`ph-cvs-antihypertensives`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which calcium channel blocker is most likely to cause constipation and AV block?`,options:[`Amlodipine`,`Nifedipine`,`Verapamil`,`Felodipine`],answer:2,explanation:`Verapamil is the phenylalkylamine with the greatest cardiac and gastrointestinal smooth muscle selectivity, giving bradycardia, AV block and constipation. Amlodipine and the other dihydropyridines act mainly on vessels and instead cause flushing, headache, reflex tachycardia and ankle oedema.`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-antihypertensives-6`,topicId:`ph-cvs-antihypertensives`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Nitrate tolerance in a patient using transdermal glyceryl trinitrate is best prevented by:`,options:[`Increasing the patch strength weekly`,`Switching to sublingual dosing every 4 hours`,`Adding a beta blocker`,`Providing a nitrate-free interval of 8-12 hours daily`],answer:3,explanation:`Continuous exposure depletes tissue sulfhydryl groups and impairs the bioactivating enzyme ALDH2, so a daily drug-free interval, usually overnight, restores responsiveness. Raising the dose only accelerates tolerance, and round-the-clock sublingual dosing would abolish the free interval altogether.`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-antihypertensives-7`,topicId:`ph-cvs-antihypertensives`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Prolonged infusion of sodium nitroprusside for a hypertensive emergency risks toxicity from:`,options:[`Cyanide and thiocyanate`,`Methaemoglobin only`,`Fluoride ions`,`Free radical nitric oxide excess`],answer:0,explanation:`Each nitroprusside molecule releases five cyanide ions, which are converted to thiocyanate by the liver and excreted renally, so accumulation causes metabolic acidosis, confusion and hypothyroidism. Sodium thiosulphate or hydroxocobalamin is given as prophylaxis or treatment; fluoride toxicity belongs to methoxyflurane, not nitroprusside.`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-antihypertensives-8`,topicId:`ph-cvs-antihypertensives`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A patient with nocturnal chest pain and ST elevation during the attack, with normal coronary angiography, should NOT receive:`,options:[`Propranolol`,`Sublingual nitrates`,`Diltiazem`,`Amlodipine`],answer:0,explanation:`This is variant (Prinzmetal) angina due to coronary spasm; a non-selective beta blocker removes beta-2 mediated coronary dilation and leaves alpha-1 vasoconstriction unopposed, worsening the spasm. Calcium channel blockers and nitrates are the treatments of choice because both relax coronary smooth muscle directly.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics-1`,topicId:`ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Digoxin produces its positive inotropic effect by inhibiting:`,options:[`Sodium-potassium ATPase`,`The sodium-calcium exchanger directly`,`Phosphodiesterase-3`,`The L-type calcium channel`],answer:0,explanation:`Blocking the Na-K ATPase raises intracellular sodium, which reduces the driving force for the sodium-calcium exchanger and so raises intracellular calcium and contractile force. The exchanger is affected only indirectly, which makes naming it as the direct target the classic near-miss; phosphodiesterase-3 inhibition is the mechanism of milrinone, not digoxin.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics-2`,topicId:`ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`In the Vaughan Williams classification, class III antiarrhythmics act by blocking:`,options:[`Potassium channels`,`Beta adrenergic receptors`,`Sodium channels`,`Calcium channels`],answer:0,explanation:`Class III drugs such as amiodarone, sotalol and dofetilide block potassium channels, prolonging repolarisation, the action potential duration and the QT interval. Calcium channel block is class IV (verapamil, diltiazem), which acts on nodal tissue rather than prolonging repolarisation.`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics-3`,topicId:`ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which electrolyte disturbance most commonly precipitates digoxin toxicity?`,options:[`Hyperkalaemia`,`Hypokalaemia`,`Hyponatraemia`,`Hypercalcaemia only`],answer:1,explanation:`Potassium and digoxin compete for the same site on the Na-K ATPase, so a low potassium level increases digoxin binding and toxicity — most often caused by a co-prescribed thiazide or loop diuretic. Hypercalcaemia does aggravate toxicity, but hypokalaemia is far and away the commonest precipitant in practice.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics-4`,topicId:`ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which drug used in chronic heart failure improves symptoms but has NOT been shown to improve survival?`,options:[`Enalapril`,`Bisoprolol`,`Digoxin`,`Spironolactone`],answer:2,explanation:`Digoxin reduces symptoms and hospital admissions but has a neutral effect on mortality, which is why it is no longer first line. ACE inhibitors, evidence-based beta blockers, mineralocorticoid antagonists and SGLT2 inhibitors are the four classes that prolong life.`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics-5`,topicId:`ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The drug of choice for terminating paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia is:`,options:[`Adenosine`,`Amiodarone`,`Lignocaine`,`Digoxin`],answer:0,explanation:`Adenosine acts on A1 receptors to block AV nodal conduction and terminates the re-entry circuit within seconds, with a half-life under 10 seconds so any adverse effect is fleeting. Lignocaine is the trap: it is a ventricular drug and is essentially useless in supraventricular arrhythmias.`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics-6`,topicId:`ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Torsades de pointes precipitated by a QT-prolonging drug is treated first with:`,options:[`Intravenous magnesium sulphate`,`Intravenous amiodarone`,`Oral sotalol`,`Digoxin`],answer:0,explanation:`Magnesium sulphate suppresses the early after-depolarisations that trigger torsades and is effective even when serum magnesium is normal. Amiodarone and sotalol both prolong the QT interval further, so giving them would worsen the very abnormality causing the arrhythmia.`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics-7`,topicId:`ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A patient with Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome develops atrial fibrillation. Which drug is contraindicated?`,options:[`Procainamide`,`Ibutilide`,`Flecainide`,`Verapamil`],answer:3,explanation:`Verapamil blocks the AV node, so atrial impulses are diverted down the accessory pathway and the ventricular rate can accelerate into ventricular fibrillation; digoxin and adenosine are dangerous for the same reason. Procainamide and flecainide are appropriate because they prolong refractoriness in the accessory pathway itself, and ibutilide is likewise acceptable. Note that in a haemodynamically unstable patient the answer is not a drug at all — it is immediate direct current cardioversion.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics-8`,topicId:`ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A patient on long-term amiodarone develops exertional dyspnoea with bilateral interstitial shadowing on chest radiograph. The most likely diagnosis is:`,options:[`Amiodarone-induced thyrotoxicosis`,`Pulmonary fibrosis due to amiodarone`,`Corneal microdeposits`,`Torsades de pointes`],answer:1,explanation:`Pulmonary fibrosis and pneumonitis are the most serious long-term amiodarone toxicities and present exactly as progressive dyspnoea with interstitial infiltrates. Thyroid dysfunction is common too but would give weight and pulse changes rather than interstitial lung shadowing.`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation-1`,topicId:`ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Statins lower LDL cholesterol mainly by:`,options:[`Binding bile acids in the intestine`,`Inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase and up-regulating hepatic LDL receptors`,`Activating PPAR-alpha and lipoprotein lipase`,`Blocking the NPC1L1 sterol transporter`],answer:1,explanation:`Inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase lowers hepatocyte cholesterol, which triggers up-regulation of LDL receptors that clear LDL from plasma. Activating PPAR-alpha is the fibrate mechanism and blocking the NPC1L1 sterol transporter is ezetimibe; both lower LDL far less than statins do, and binding bile acids in the intestine is the resin mechanism.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation-2`,topicId:`ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The antidote for heparin overdose is:`,options:[`Vitamin K`,`Protamine sulphate`,`Tranexamic acid`,`Idarucizumab`],answer:1,explanation:`Protamine is a strongly basic protein that binds acidic heparin and neutralises it instantly, dose being about 1 mg per 100 units of heparin. Vitamin K is the trap answer taken from warfarin, and it has no effect at all on heparin.`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation-3`,topicId:`ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Warfarin is monitored by INR while heparin is monitored by aPTT because warfarin:`,options:[`Acts only in vitro`,`Activates antithrombin III`,`Directly inhibits thrombin`,`Inhibits vitamin K dependent gamma-carboxylation of factors II, VII, IX and X`],answer:3,explanation:`Warfarin blocks vitamin K epoxide reductase, so factors II, VII, IX and X and proteins C and S cannot be carboxylated; factor VII has the shortest half-life, so the extrinsic pathway and hence the prothrombin time and INR are affected first. Activation of antithrombin III is the heparin mechanism, which prolongs the aPTT instead.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation-4`,topicId:`ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Low-dose aspirin is antithrombotic because it:`,options:[`Reversibly inhibits COX-2 in the platelet`,`Irreversibly acetylates platelet COX-1 for the platelet lifespan`,`Blocks the P2Y12 ADP receptor`,`Inhibits glycoprotein IIb/IIIa`],answer:1,explanation:`Aspirin acetylates a serine residue on platelet COX-1 irreversibly, and since the anucleate platelet cannot make new enzyme, thromboxane A2 synthesis stays blocked for the 7-10 day platelet lifespan. The P2Y12 receptor is the target of clopidogrel and ticagrelor, a completely separate pathway of platelet activation.`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation-5`,topicId:`ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which hypolipidaemic drug produces the greatest rise in HDL cholesterol?`,options:[`Ezetimibe`,`Cholestyramine`,`Niacin`,`Atorvastatin`],answer:2,explanation:`Niacin raises HDL by 20-35 percent, more than any other agent, and is the only drug that appreciably lowers lipoprotein(a). Cholestyramine is the poorest choice here because it lowers LDL only modestly and can actually raise triglycerides.`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation-6`,topicId:`ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Heparin rather than warfarin is used for anticoagulation in pregnancy because heparin:`,options:[`Has a longer duration of action`,`Does not require monitoring`,`Can be given orally`,`Is a large polar molecule that does not cross the placenta`],answer:3,explanation:`Heparin is a large, highly charged mucopolysaccharide that cannot cross the placenta, so the fetus is unexposed. Warfarin is small and lipid soluble and causes warfarin embryopathy in the first trimester and fetal haemorrhage later.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation-7`,topicId:`ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`On day 7 of unfractionated heparin the platelet count falls from 250,000 to 90,000 per microlitre and the patient develops a limb thrombosis. The correct management is:`,options:[`Continue heparin and transfuse platelets`,`Stop heparin and start warfarin immediately`,`Stop all heparin and start argatroban or another direct thrombin inhibitor`,`Switch to low molecular weight heparin`],answer:2,explanation:`This is type II heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, an immune reaction to the heparin-platelet factor 4 complex that paradoxically causes thrombosis, so all heparin including LMWH and flushes must stop and a direct thrombin inhibitor is substituted. Starting warfarin at this point is dangerous because early protein C depletion can cause venous limb gangrene.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation-8`,topicId:`ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A patient stable on warfarin with an INR of 2.5 is started on rifampicin for tuberculosis. The expected effect is:`,options:[`INR rises, requiring a dose reduction`,`INR falls, requiring a dose increase`,`INR is unchanged because warfarin is renally cleared`,`INR rises transiently then falls below 1`],answer:1,explanation:`Rifampicin is a powerful inducer of CYP2C9 and CYP3A4, so warfarin is metabolised faster, the INR falls and the dose must be raised — this is an ADR type F, therapeutic failure. Inhibitors such as metronidazole, cotrimoxazole and fluconazole do the opposite and push the INR up.`},{id:`q-ph-general-principles-9`,topicId:`ph-general-principles`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A drug that occupies all available receptors yet still produces only 40 percent of the maximal tissue response is best described as having:`,options:[`Intrinsic activity between 0 and 1 (partial agonist)`,`Intrinsic activity of 1 (full agonist)`,`Intrinsic activity of 0 (pure antagonist)`,`Negative intrinsic activity (inverse agonist)`],answer:0,explanation:`A partial agonist has intrinsic activity between 0 and 1, so even complete occupancy gives a submaximal ceiling response — buprenorphine at the mu receptor is the standard example. An inverse agonist is the tempting choice, but it has negative intrinsic activity and drives the receptor below its constitutive baseline instead of producing a partial response.`},{id:`q-ph-general-principles-10`,topicId:`ph-general-principles`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which of the following is a prodrug that must be hydrolysed in the liver before it can act?`,options:[`Lisinopril`,`Captopril`,`Enalapril`,`Atenolol`],answer:2,explanation:`Enalapril is an ester that is de-esterified to enalaprilat, the active diacid, which is why its onset is slower than that of captopril. Lisinopril is the trap because it belongs to the same class yet is active as administered and needs no metabolic conversion at all.`},{id:`q-ph-general-principles-11`,topicId:`ph-general-principles`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The certain safety factor (CSF), a stricter index of safety than the therapeutic index, is calculated as:`,options:[`LD50 / ED50`,`LD1 / ED99`,`ED99 / LD1`,`LD99 / ED1`],answer:1,explanation:`The certain safety factor is LD1 / ED99 — the dose lethal to 1 percent divided by the dose effective in 99 percent, so it compares the overlapping tails of the two curves. LD50 / ED50 is the ordinary therapeutic index, which can look reassuringly large even when the extremes of the curves overlap and some patients are harmed at effective doses.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-ph-general-principles-12`,topicId:`ph-general-principles`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A patient prepared with phenoxybenzamine before adrenalectomy stays hypotensive despite very large doses of noradrenaline. The reason is that phenoxybenzamine:`,options:[`Competes reversibly with noradrenaline at the same alpha receptor site`,`Forms a covalent bond with the alpha receptor, making the blockade insurmountable`,`Depletes noradrenaline from sympathetic nerve terminals`,`Acts at a different receptor to produce an opposing physiological effect`],answer:1,explanation:`Phenoxybenzamine alkylates the alpha receptor covalently, so the effective receptor pool shrinks, Emax is depressed and no concentration of agonist can restore the response until new receptors are synthesised. Reversible competition describes phentolamine, whose blockade is surmountable simply by raising the noradrenaline concentration.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-general-principles-13`,topicId:`ph-general-principles`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A patient taking doxycycline with milk shows a marked fall in antibiotic absorption. This interaction is an example of:`,options:[`Enzyme induction`,`Physiological antagonism`,`Competitive receptor antagonism`,`Chemical antagonism by chelation`],answer:3,explanation:`Calcium chelates the tetracycline nucleus to form an insoluble, unabsorbable complex, so the two substances react chemically in the gut lumen — the same principle as dimercaprol chelating arsenic. Enzyme induction is wrong because nothing here alters metabolism; the drug never reaches the circulation to be metabolised.`},{id:`q-ph-general-principles-14`,topicId:`ph-general-principles`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A healthy young man remains apnoeic for three hours after a single normal dose of succinylcholine. This response is classified as:`,options:[`Tolerance`,`Tachyphylaxis`,`Idiosyncrasy`,`Type I hypersensitivity`],answer:2,explanation:`Prolonged apnoea reflects an inherited atypical pseudocholinesterase with a low dibucaine number that cannot hydrolyse the drug — a genetically determined, qualitatively abnormal response, which is idiosyncrasy. Type I hypersensitivity is excluded because there is no prior sensitisation, no IgE involvement and none of the urticaria or bronchospasm that would accompany an allergic reaction.`},{id:`q-ph-general-principles-15`,topicId:`ph-general-principles`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A competitive antagonist at a concentration of 1 x 10^-8 M produces an agonist dose ratio of 101. Its pA2 value is:`,options:[`6`,`8`,`10`,`12`],answer:2,explanation:`From the Schild relation, dose ratio minus 1 equals [B] / KB, so 100 = 10^-8 / KB and KB = 10^-10 M; pA2 is the negative logarithm of that dissociation constant, giving 10. The value 8 is the trap from simply taking the negative log of the antagonist concentration used, which is correct only when the dose ratio is exactly 2.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-ph-general-principles-16`,topicId:`ph-general-principles`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A patient who is a CYP2D6 poor metaboliser obtains no analgesia from codeine. The explanation is that codeine:`,options:[`Is a prodrug requiring O-demethylation by CYP2D6 to morphine`,`Is displaced from plasma protein binding sites by CYP2D6`,`Is inactivated solely by CYP2D6, so its levels become too high`,`Cannot cross the blood-brain barrier unless CYP2D6 is active`],answer:0,explanation:`Codeine has very low affinity for the mu receptor; roughly 10 percent is O-demethylated by CYP2D6 to morphine, which provides the analgesia, so a poor metaboliser feels nothing while an ultra-rapid metaboliser risks respiratory depression. Saying CYP2D6 only inactivates codeine reverses the direction of the reaction and would wrongly predict stronger, not absent, analgesia.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-ans-cholinergic-9`,topicId:`ph-ans-cholinergic`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Pilocarpine lowers intraocular pressure in angle-closure glaucoma because it is a:`,options:[`Alpha-1 agonist causing mydriasis`,`Beta blocker that reduces aqueous humour secretion`,`Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor`,`Muscarinic agonist causing miosis and ciliary muscle contraction`],answer:3,explanation:`Pilocarpine directly stimulates muscarinic receptors; contraction of the sphincter pupillae pulls the iris out of the angle and ciliary muscle contraction opens the trabecular meshwork, so aqueous drains. Timolol also lowers pressure but by reducing aqueous formation, and it leaves pupil size unchanged, which is why it cannot break an acute angle-closure attack the same way.`},{id:`q-ph-ans-cholinergic-10`,topicId:`ph-ans-cholinergic`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The anticholinesterase used for long-term symptomatic treatment of myasthenia gravis is:`,options:[`Edrophonium`,`Physostigmine`,`Pyridostigmine`,`Echothiophate`],answer:2,explanation:`Pyridostigmine is a quaternary carbamate acting for 3-6 hours with less gastrointestinal upset than neostigmine, which suits chronic oral dosing. Edrophonium is the classic distractor, but it binds only electrostatically and its effect lasts a few minutes, restricting it to diagnostic use.`},{id:`q-ph-ans-cholinergic-11`,topicId:`ph-ans-cholinergic`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Succinylcholine is avoided from about 24 hours after extensive burns, spinal cord injury or crush injury because of the risk of:`,options:[`Prolonged phase II block`,`Malignant hyperthermia`,`Severe bradycardia from muscarinic stimulation`,`Hyperkalaemic cardiac arrest`],answer:3,explanation:`Denervated or burnt muscle up-regulates extrajunctional nicotinic receptors over the whole fibre, so depolarisation dumps a large potassium load into the circulation and can arrest the heart. Malignant hyperthermia is a real succinylcholine hazard but it depends on an inherited ryanodine receptor defect and has nothing to do with burns or denervation.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-ans-cholinergic-12`,topicId:`ph-ans-cholinergic`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which agent reverses rocuronium blockade by encapsulating the drug molecule rather than by inhibiting acetylcholinesterase?`,options:[`Neostigmine`,`Sugammadex`,`Pralidoxime`,`Edrophonium`],answer:1,explanation:`Sugammadex is a modified gamma-cyclodextrin that traps rocuronium and vecuronium in plasma, giving rapid and complete reversal with no muscarinic consequences. Neostigmine reverses by raising acetylcholine at the end plate and therefore must be combined with atropine or glycopyrrolate to prevent bradycardia, salivation and colic.`},{id:`q-ph-ans-cholinergic-13`,topicId:`ph-ans-cholinergic`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`As the dose of atropine is progressively increased, which effect appears FIRST?`,options:[`Dryness of mouth with reduced sweating`,`Cycloplegia and mydriasis`,`Urinary retention`,`Delirium and hallucination`],answer:0,explanation:`Salivary, bronchial and sweat glands are the most sensitive tissues, so dry mouth and inhibition of sweating appear at doses around 0.5 mg, followed by tachycardia, then the eye, then the bladder, and only at high doses the central effects. Cycloplegia and mydriasis need far larger systemic doses, which is why an antisecretory dose of atropine seldom blurs vision.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-ans-cholinergic-14`,topicId:`ph-ans-cholinergic`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`An 80-year-old man on oxybutynin for overactive bladder becomes confused and forgetful. The most appropriate substitution is:`,options:[`Hyoscine (scopolamine)`,`Atropine`,`Trospium chloride`,`Dicyclomine`],answer:2,explanation:`Trospium is a quaternary ammonium antimuscarinic that barely crosses the blood-brain barrier, so bladder efficacy is retained without the central anticholinergic burden of the tertiary amine oxybutynin. Hyoscine would be the worst possible swap because it is the most centrally penetrant antimuscarinic of the group and causes sedation and amnesia.`},{id:`q-ph-ans-cholinergic-15`,topicId:`ph-ans-cholinergic`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Very small doses of atropine can transiently SLOW the heart. The accepted explanation is:`,options:[`Blockade of presynaptic M1 autoreceptors, which increases acetylcholine release at the sinoatrial node`,`Partial agonist activity of atropine at cardiac M2 receptors`,`Baroreceptor reflex activation following a fall in blood pressure`,`Direct inhibition of sinoatrial node sodium channels`],answer:0,explanation:`Low concentrations preferentially block the inhibitory presynaptic M1 autoreceptors on vagal endings, removing the brake on acetylcholine release, so nodal M2 stimulation briefly increases before postjunctional blockade takes over and the rate rises. Atropine has no intrinsic agonist activity whatever, so describing it as a partial agonist at M2 misstates its pharmacology.`},{id:`q-ph-ans-cholinergic-16`,topicId:`ph-ans-cholinergic`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A patient with myasthenia gravis on a high dose of pyridostigmine develops worsening weakness with salivation, sweating, abdominal colic and constricted pupils. The correct action is:`,options:[`Increase the pyridostigmine dose`,`Give intravenous neostigmine`,`Withhold the anticholinesterase, give atropine and support ventilation`,`Give pralidoxime`],answer:2,explanation:`The florid muscarinic overactivity identifies cholinergic crisis, in which excess acetylcholine produces a depolarising block, so the anticholinesterase is stopped, atropine controls the secretions and ventilation is supported. Increasing the dose would be right only in myasthenic crisis, where weakness comes with a dry mouth and dilated pupils rather than these secretions.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-ans-adrenergic-9`,topicId:`ph-ans-adrenergic`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which alpha blocker is preferred for benign prostatic hyperplasia because it causes the least postural hypotension?`,options:[`Prazosin`,`Tamsulosin`,`Phentolamine`,`Phenoxybenzamine`],answer:1,explanation:`Tamsulosin is selective for the alpha-1A subtype that predominates in the prostate and bladder neck, so it relieves obstruction with little effect on vascular alpha-1B receptors. Prazosin relaxes the prostate too but blocks alpha-1 non-selectively and is notorious for first-dose syncope.`},{id:`q-ph-ans-adrenergic-10`,topicId:`ph-ans-adrenergic`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Clonidine lowers blood pressure principally by stimulating:`,options:[`Peripheral alpha-1 receptors`,`Cardiac beta-1 receptors`,`Central alpha-2 receptors, reducing sympathetic outflow`,`Peripheral beta-2 receptors`],answer:2,explanation:`Clonidine is an alpha-2 agonist acting in the brainstem vasomotor centre; reduced sympathetic outflow lowers heart rate, cardiac output and peripheral resistance. Stimulating peripheral alpha-1 receptors would raise blood pressure, and indeed a rapid intravenous injection of clonidine produces exactly that transient pressor effect before the central action dominates.`},{id:`q-ph-ans-adrenergic-11`,topicId:`ph-ans-adrenergic`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A noradrenaline infusion extravasates and the surrounding skin becomes pale and cold. The correct treatment is:`,options:[`Local infiltration of adrenaline`,`Topical propranolol`,`Intravenous atropine`,`Local infiltration of phentolamine`],answer:3,explanation:`Intense alpha-1 vasoconstriction is causing ischaemia, so the area is infiltrated with the alpha blocker phentolamine to restore perfusion before necrosis develops. Infiltrating more adrenaline would deepen the vasoconstriction, and a beta blocker does nothing to the alpha-mediated spasm.`},{id:`q-ph-ans-adrenergic-12`,topicId:`ph-ans-adrenergic`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A diabetic on insulin is started on propranolol and later has an unrecognised severe hypoglycaemic episode. The main hazard of beta blockers here is that they:`,options:[`Mask tachycardia and tremor and delay recovery by blunting glycogenolysis`,`Increase insulin secretion from the pancreas`,`Increase hepatic gluconeogenesis`,`Abolish sweating, the only remaining warning sign`],answer:0,explanation:`Beta blockade removes the adrenergic warning symptoms of palpitations and tremor and also impairs beta-2 mediated hepatic glycogenolysis, so hypoglycaemia is both unnoticed and prolonged. Sweating is the one warning that persists because it is cholinergically mediated, so claiming it is abolished is exactly the wrong way round.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-ans-adrenergic-13`,topicId:`ph-ans-adrenergic`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which beta blocker is beta-1 selective and additionally vasodilates by releasing nitric oxide?`,options:[`Carvedilol`,`Labetalol`,`Nebivolol`,`Esmolol`],answer:2,explanation:`Nebivolol is the most beta-1 selective agent in clinical use and its endothelial nitric oxide release adds vasodilatation with a favourable metabolic profile. Carvedilol and labetalol also vasodilate, but they do so by blocking alpha-1 receptors and both are non-selective at the beta receptor.`},{id:`q-ph-ans-adrenergic-14`,topicId:`ph-ans-adrenergic`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A child given repeated nebulised salbutamol for acute severe asthma develops tremor and a serum potassium of 2.9 mmol/L. The mechanism is:`,options:[`Renal potassium wasting caused by beta-2 stimulation`,`Beta-2 stimulation of the Na-K ATPase driving potassium into cells`,`Vomiting induced by the drug`,`Metabolic acidosis shifting potassium out of cells`],answer:1,explanation:`Beta-2 agonists activate the Na-K ATPase in skeletal muscle, so potassium moves from plasma into cells; total body potassium is unchanged, and salbutamol nebulisation is used deliberately for this effect in hyperkalaemia. Acidosis would drive potassium out of cells and raise the plasma level, the opposite of what is seen here.`},{id:`q-ph-ans-adrenergic-15`,topicId:`ph-ans-adrenergic`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Reserpine depletes tissue noradrenaline stores by:`,options:[`Blocking neuronal uptake-1`,`Inhibiting tyrosine hydroxylase`,`Preventing exocytotic release of the transmitter`,`Irreversibly inhibiting the vesicular monoamine transporter (VMAT-2)`],answer:3,explanation:`Reserpine blocks VMAT-2, so the amine cannot be packaged into vesicles and cytoplasmic MAO destroys it, emptying the stores over hours and leaving depletion that lasts until new vesicles are made. Inhibiting tyrosine hydroxylase is the action of metirosine, and blocking exocytotic release is what guanethidine does — neither of these destroys the existing stores.`},{id:`q-ph-ans-adrenergic-16`,topicId:`ph-ans-adrenergic`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Esmolol is chosen to control intraoperative tachycardia because it is:`,options:[`Hydrolysed by red blood cell esterases, giving a half-life of about 9 minutes`,`Excreted unchanged by the kidney within minutes`,`A partial agonist with intrinsic sympathomimetic activity`,`A non-selective blocker with strong membrane stabilising activity`],answer:0,explanation:`The ester side chain of esmolol is cleaved by erythrocyte esterases independently of liver and kidney, so the half-life is roughly 9 minutes and the infusion can be titrated or stopped within minutes. It is beta-1 selective with no intrinsic sympathomimetic and no membrane stabilising activity, so those descriptions belong to pindolol and propranolol respectively.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics-9`,topicId:`ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The drug of choice for trigeminal neuralgia is:`,options:[`Carbamazepine`,`Phenytoin`,`Morphine`,`Diazepam`],answer:0,explanation:`Carbamazepine gives useful relief in about 70 percent of patients by producing use-dependent sodium channel blockade in the trigeminal nucleus; oxcarbazepine is the usual alternative. Morphine is a poor answer because neuropathic lancinating pain responds badly to opioids compared with sodium channel blockers.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics-10`,topicId:`ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which benzodiazepine is water soluble in its acidic injectable formulation and preferred for short procedural sedation?`,options:[`Diazepam`,`Chlordiazepoxide`,`Midazolam`,`Clonazepam`],answer:2,explanation:`Midazolam is formulated at acid pH where its open ring form is water soluble, then closes to a lipophilic form at blood pH, giving rapid onset and a short 2-4 hour half-life with useful anterograde amnesia. Diazepam is the trap because it needs propylene glycol solubilisation, causes thrombophlebitis and has long-acting metabolites lasting days.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics-11`,topicId:`ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A 24-year-old woman with generalised tonic-clonic and myoclonic seizures is planning a pregnancy. Which antiepileptic should be avoided?`,options:[`Levetiracetam`,`Lamotrigine`,`Clobazam`,`Sodium valproate`],answer:3,explanation:`Valproate carries the highest teratogenic risk of the common antiepileptics, causing neural tube defects in 1-2 percent and reduced childhood IQ, so it is avoided in women of childbearing potential unless nothing else works. Lamotrigine and levetiracetam are the preferred alternatives for this seizure pattern because both have far lower malformation rates.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics-12`,topicId:`ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Gum hypertrophy, hirsutism, coarsening of facial features and megaloblastic anaemia are characteristic adverse effects of:`,options:[`Valproate`,`Phenytoin`,`Vigabatrin`,`Levetiracetam`],answer:1,explanation:`These are the classic chronic toxicities of phenytoin, the megaloblastic anaemia arising from interference with folate metabolism and treated with folic acid. Valproate is the tempting alternative but its signature effects are the opposite cosmetically — weight gain, hair loss and tremor — together with hepatotoxicity.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics-13`,topicId:`ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which antiepileptic raises brain GABA by irreversibly inhibiting GABA transaminase?`,options:[`Tiagabine`,`Gabapentin`,`Vigabatrin`,`Topiramate`],answer:2,explanation:`Vigabatrin is a suicide substrate for GABA transaminase, so GABA cannot be degraded and its concentration rises; the price is irreversible concentric visual field constriction, which limits its use to infantile spasms and refractory cases. Tiagabine also raises synaptic GABA but does so by blocking the GAT-1 reuptake transporter, a completely reversible mechanism.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics-14`,topicId:`ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Zolpidem is hypnotic but has little muscle relaxant or anticonvulsant action because it:`,options:[`Binds selectively to GABA-A receptors containing the alpha-1 subunit`,`Acts on the barbiturate site of the GABA-A receptor`,`Blocks histamine H1 receptors instead of GABA receptors`,`Is a melatonin MT1 and MT2 agonist`],answer:0,explanation:`Zolpidem is an imidazopyridine that binds preferentially to the alpha-1 (BZ1) subtype that mediates sedation, so it spares the alpha-2 and alpha-3 subtypes responsible for muscle relaxation and anticonvulsant effect. Melatonin receptor agonism is the mechanism of ramelteon, an entirely different hypnotic class that flumazenil cannot reverse.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics-15`,topicId:`ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A patient on phenytoin 300 mg/day has a steady-state level of 6 mcg/mL. If Vmax is 500 mg/day and Km is 4 mcg/mL, the daily dose needed for a level of 15 mcg/mL is about:`,options:[`750 mg/day`,`395 mg/day`,`500 mg/day`,`320 mg/day`],answer:1,explanation:`Michaelis-Menten dosing gives dose rate = Vmax x Css / (Km + Css) = 500 x 15 / (4 + 15) = 7500 / 19, which is about 395 mg/day. Check the parameters against the starting point: 500 x 6 / (4 + 6) = 300 mg/day, which is exactly the dose given, so the figures are internally consistent. The figure 750 mg/day is the classic error of scaling linearly from the present dose (300 x 15 / 6), which would be valid only if phenytoin followed first-order kinetics — it does not, which is why small dose increases cause disproportionate jumps in level.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics-16`,topicId:`ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A child on sodium valproate becomes vomiting, drowsy and confused. Transaminases are normal but plasma ammonia is high. The mechanism is:`,options:[`Acute hepatic necrosis`,`Drug-induced hyponatraemia`,`Accumulation of an epoxide metabolite`,`Carnitine depletion inhibiting the urea cycle, causing hyperammonaemia`],answer:3,explanation:`Valproate sequesters carnitine and its metabolites inhibit carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I, so ammonia accumulates and produces encephalopathy even when liver enzymes are normal; treatment is to stop the drug and give L-carnitine. Hyponatraemia is the trap taken from carbamazepine, and it would present with a low sodium rather than a high ammonia.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants-9`,topicId:`ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which atypical antipsychotic is most likely to cause galactorrhoea and amenorrhoea?`,options:[`Clozapine`,`Quetiapine`,`Risperidone`,`Aripiprazole`],answer:2,explanation:`Risperidone produces tight, dose-related D2 blockade in the tuberoinfundibular pathway and raises prolactin more than any other atypical agent. Aripiprazole is the opposite extreme because, as a D2 partial agonist, it maintains enough dopamine tone to keep prolactin normal or even lower it.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants-10`,topicId:`ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Fluoxetine and sertraline relieve depression by:`,options:[`Inhibiting the presynaptic serotonin transporter (SERT)`,`Blocking postsynaptic 5-HT2A receptors`,`Inhibiting monoamine oxidase A`,`Blocking the noradrenaline transporter`],answer:0,explanation:`SSRIs block SERT, so serotonin removed from the synapse is reduced and synaptic concentration rises. Blocking 5-HT2A receptors is a feature of mirtazapine and the atypical antipsychotics and would if anything reduce serotonergic signalling at that receptor, so it cannot explain the antidepressant action of an SSRI.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants-11`,topicId:`ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A patient on fluoxetine is given tramadol and within hours develops agitation, diarrhoea, dilated pupils, hyperreflexia and inducible ankle clonus with a temperature of 39 C. The diagnosis is:`,options:[`Neuroleptic malignant syndrome`,`Serotonin syndrome`,`Malignant hyperthermia`,`Anticholinergic toxidrome`],answer:1,explanation:`Onset within hours with clonus, hyperreflexia, mydriasis and diarrhoea is the neuromuscular and gastrointestinal signature of serotonin excess; management is withdrawal of the serotonergic drugs plus cyproheptadine. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome is the near-miss but it evolves over days after a dopamine blocker and shows lead-pipe rigidity with hyporeflexia and normal or small pupils, never clonus.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants-12`,topicId:`ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A patient who has taken an overdose of amitriptyline is hypotensive with a QRS duration of 140 ms. The most important specific measure is:`,options:[`Flumazenil`,`Physostigmine as the first-line antidote`,`Oral activated charcoal alone`,`Intravenous sodium bicarbonate`],answer:3,explanation:`Tricyclic cardiotoxicity comes from fast sodium channel blockade; sodium bicarbonate raises extracellular sodium and alkalinises plasma, narrowing the QRS and correcting the hypotension and arrhythmia. Physostigmine may reverse the antimuscarinic delirium but it can provoke asystole and seizures in tricyclic overdose, so it is not the first-line measure.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants-13`,topicId:`ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which antidepressant is contraindicated in a patient with epilepsy or bulimia nervosa?`,options:[`Bupropion`,`Sertraline`,`Mirtazapine`,`Duloxetine`],answer:0,explanation:`Bupropion lowers the seizure threshold in a dose-dependent way, and the electrolyte disturbance of an eating disorder magnifies that risk, so both conditions are contraindications. Sertraline is the tempting alternative to reject, but SSRIs carry only a small seizure risk and are used routinely in bulimia, fluoxetine being licensed for it.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants-14`,topicId:`ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A washout of about five weeks is required after stopping fluoxetine before starting an MAO inhibitor because:`,options:[`Fluoxetine irreversibly inhibits monoamine oxidase itself`,`Fluoxetine is more than 95 percent protein bound`,`Its active metabolite norfluoxetine has a half-life of 7-15 days`,`Fluoxetine is excreted unchanged and accumulates in renal impairment`],answer:2,explanation:`Fluoxetine is demethylated to norfluoxetine, which is equally active and has a half-life of one to two weeks, so five half-lives take roughly five weeks; other SSRIs need only about two weeks. High protein binding is a real property of fluoxetine but binding does not determine how long a drug persists after the last dose — elimination half-life does.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants-15`,topicId:`ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A patient on long-term lithium passes 5 litres of dilute urine daily and does not respond to desmopressin. Lithium must be continued. Which drug reduces the urine volume without pushing the serum lithium towards toxicity?`,options:[`Desmopressin at a higher dose`,`Amiloride`,`Hydrochlorothiazide`,`Furosemide`],answer:1,explanation:`Lithium enters collecting duct cells through the epithelial sodium channel and blunts the response to antidiuretic hormone, so amiloride blocks that channel, protects the cell and improves the nephrogenic diabetes insipidus. A thiazide would also reduce urine volume but it raises lithium levels towards toxicity, and desmopressin is useless because the defect is renal resistance, not hormone deficiency.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants-16`,topicId:`ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Antidepressants block monoamine reuptake within hours, yet mood improves only after 2-4 weeks. The best explanation is:`,options:[`Slow accumulation of the drug in body fat`,`Progressive induction of hepatic enzymes`,`The time needed for the drug to cross the blood-brain barrier`,`Gradual desensitisation of presynaptic 5-HT1A autoreceptors with adaptive receptor down-regulation`],answer:3,explanation:`The immediate rise in synaptic serotonin first activates inhibitory somatodendritic 5-HT1A autoreceptors and shuts down raphe firing; only when these desensitise over two to three weeks does neuronal firing recover and transmission genuinely increase, alongside down-regulation of postsynaptic beta and 5-HT2 receptors. Delayed entry into the brain is wrong because these lipophilic drugs reach the central nervous system within an hour of the first dose.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics-9`,topicId:`ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which opioid is used for oral substitution (maintenance) therapy in heroin dependence?`,options:[`Methadone`,`Pethidine`,`Fentanyl`,`Naloxone`],answer:0,explanation:`Methadone is orally effective with a long half-life of 24-36 hours, so a single daily dose prevents withdrawal without producing the peaks that reinforce drug-seeking; buprenorphine is the partial-agonist alternative. Naloxone is a pure antagonist and would precipitate acute withdrawal rather than prevent it.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics-10`,topicId:`ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which of the following local anaesthetics is an amide?`,options:[`Procaine`,`Lignocaine`,`Benzocaine`,`Tetracaine`],answer:1,explanation:`Lignocaine is an amide, metabolised in the liver by CYP enzymes and rarely allergenic; a quick check is that amide names contain two letter i sounds, as in lignocaine, bupivacaine and prilocaine. The other three are esters, hydrolysed by plasma cholinesterase to para-aminobenzoic acid, which is the usual cause of local anaesthetic allergy.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics-11`,topicId:`ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A patient taking phenelzine is given an opioid for renal colic and develops hyperthermia, rigidity, agitation and hypertension. The opioid most likely responsible is:`,options:[`Morphine`,`Fentanyl`,`Pethidine`,`Codeine`],answer:2,explanation:`Pethidine also blocks serotonin reuptake, so with an MAO inhibitor it produces an excitatory serotonergic reaction with hyperthermia and rigidity and is absolutely contraindicated. Morphine is the safer choice in a patient on an MAOI because it has no serotonin reuptake activity, though the dose is still reduced.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics-12`,topicId:`ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A heroin overdose responds to a bolus of naloxone, but 45 minutes later the patient is again drowsy with a respiratory rate of 6 per minute. The reason is that naloxone:`,options:[`Produces tolerance within minutes`,`Was neutralised by the opioid still in the stomach`,`Has precipitated a withdrawal reaction`,`Has a shorter half-life than the opioid it is reversing`],answer:3,explanation:`Naloxone acts for only about 30-60 minutes, whereas heroin, morphine and especially methadone last far longer, so re-narcotisation is expected and repeat doses or an infusion are needed. Tolerance is not the answer because opioid antagonists do not induce tolerance to themselves over so short a period.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics-13`,topicId:`ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Bupivacaine is never used for intravenous regional anaesthesia (Bier block) because it:`,options:[`Causes methaemoglobinaemia`,`Is markedly cardiotoxic, dissociating slowly from cardiac sodium channels`,`Has too short a duration of action`,`Is hydrolysed by plasma cholinesterase before it can act`],answer:1,explanation:`Bupivacaine binds cardiac sodium channels avidly and leaves them slowly, so accidental systemic release causes refractory ventricular arrhythmia and cardiac arrest that is very hard to resuscitate; intravenous lipid emulsion is the rescue. Methaemoglobinaemia is the trap taken from prilocaine and benzocaine, whose metabolite o-toluidine oxidises haemoglobin.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics-14`,topicId:`ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which intravenous anaesthetic produces dissociative anaesthesia with analgesia and preserved respiration, but is avoided when intracranial pressure is raised?`,options:[`Ketamine`,`Propofol`,`Thiopentone`,`Etomidate`],answer:0,explanation:`Ketamine is an NMDA channel blocker that gives profound analgesia, maintains airway reflexes and raises blood pressure through sympathetic stimulation, but it increases cerebral blood flow and intracranial pressure and causes emergence delirium. Propofol does the opposite on every count — it lowers intracranial pressure and blood pressure, depresses respiration and has no analgesic action at all.`},{id:`q-ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics-15`,topicId:`ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which inhalational agent has a MAC of about 105 percent, so that it cannot by itself produce surgical anaesthesia at normal atmospheric pressure?`,options:[`Halothane`,`Isoflurane`,`Nitrous oxide`,`Sevoflurane`],answer:2,explanation:`A MAC above 100 percent means more than a whole atmosphere of the gas would be needed, so nitrous oxide (MAC about 105) is used only as a carrier and analgesic supplement, contributing the second gas effect and risking diffusion hypoxia at the end. Halothane at 0.75, isoflurane at 1.15 and sevoflurane at about 2 are all potent enough to anaesthetise alone, since a low MAC means high potency.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics-16`,topicId:`ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Etomidate gives excellent cardiovascular stability at induction, yet infusions are avoided in critically ill patients because it:`,options:[`Depresses the myocardium severely`,`Inhibits 11-beta-hydroxylase and suppresses cortisol synthesis`,`Triggers malignant hyperthermia`,`Causes dose-dependent hepatotoxicity`],answer:1,explanation:`Etomidate blocks adrenal 11-beta-hydroxylase, so cortisol production falls for up to 24 hours after even a single dose, which is why prolonged infusion in sepsis increased mortality. Severe myocardial depression is precisely what etomidate does NOT cause; that property belongs to propofol and thiopentone and is the reason etomidate is chosen in shock.`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-antihypertensives-9`,topicId:`ph-cvs-antihypertensives`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Thiazide diuretics lower blood pressure by acting on which part of the nephron?`,options:[`Na-K-2Cl symporter of the thick ascending limb`,`Na-Cl symporter of the distal convoluted tubule`,`Epithelial sodium channel of the collecting duct`,`Carbonic anhydrase of the proximal tubule`],answer:1,explanation:`Thiazides inhibit the Na-Cl symporter in the early distal convoluted tubule, giving moderate natriuresis and, on long-term use, a fall in peripheral resistance. The Na-K-2Cl symporter of the loop is the target of furosemide, which is a far more powerful diuretic but a weaker antihypertensive.`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-antihypertensives-10`,topicId:`ph-cvs-antihypertensives`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which antihypertensive is a direct renin inhibitor?`,options:[`Aliskiren`,`Candesartan`,`Ramipril`,`Sacubitril`],answer:0,explanation:`Aliskiren binds the active site of renin and blocks the conversion of angiotensinogen to angiotensin I, the very first step of the cascade. Candesartan acts much further downstream at the AT1 receptor, and sacubitril is not a renin-system drug at all but a neprilysin inhibitor.`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-antihypertensives-11`,topicId:`ph-cvs-antihypertensives`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A patient controlled on ramipril has a persistent dry cough for six weeks. The most appropriate step is to:`,options:[`Halve the ramipril dose and continue`,`Switch to enalapril`,`Switch to telmisartan`,`Add codeine linctus and continue ramipril`],answer:2,explanation:`An angiotensin receptor blocker such as telmisartan gives equivalent blood pressure and renal protection but leaves kininase II untouched, so bradykinin does not accumulate and the cough resolves. Switching to enalapril fails because the cough is a class effect of ACE inhibition, not a property of one molecule, and dose reduction rarely abolishes it.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-antihypertensives-12`,topicId:`ph-cvs-antihypertensives`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which antihypertensive causes reflex tachycardia, salt and water retention, and a lupus-like syndrome in slow acetylators?`,options:[`Minoxidil`,`Methyldopa`,`Clonidine`,`Hydralazine`],answer:3,explanation:`Hydralazine is a direct arteriolar dilator, so baroreflex activation gives tachycardia and fluid retention, and slow acetylators accumulate enough drug to develop drug-induced lupus, as they also do with procainamide and isoniazid. Methyldopa is the tempting distractor because it too causes an autoimmune problem, but that is a positive direct Coombs test with haemolytic anaemia, not a lupus syndrome.`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-antihypertensives-13`,topicId:`ph-cvs-antihypertensives`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A 52-year-old type 2 diabetic has blood pressure 150/92 mmHg and albuminuria. The preferred first-line drug is:`,options:[`Amlodipine`,`An ACE inhibitor such as ramipril`,`Hydrochlorothiazide`,`Atenolol`],answer:1,explanation:`ACE inhibitors and ARBs dilate the efferent arteriole, lower intraglomerular pressure and reduce albuminuria and progression of nephropathy over and above their blood pressure effect. Amlodipine lowers pressure just as well but has no comparable antiproteinuric action, and atenolol is a poor choice because beta blockers worsen glycaemic control and lipid profile.`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-antihypertensives-14`,topicId:`ph-cvs-antihypertensives`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which antihypertensive combination should be avoided because it increases hyperkalaemia, hypotension and acute kidney injury without added benefit?`,options:[`An ACE inhibitor with an angiotensin receptor blocker`,`An ACE inhibitor with amlodipine`,`An angiotensin receptor blocker with a thiazide`,`An ACE inhibitor with a thiazide`],answer:0,explanation:`Dual blockade of the renin-angiotensin system was tested in large trials and produced more renal failure and hyperkalaemia with no reduction in cardiovascular events, so the two classes are not combined. Pairing a renin-system drug with either a dihydropyridine or a thiazide is standard practice, and the thiazide-induced potassium loss actually offsets the potassium retention of the ACE inhibitor.`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-antihypertensives-15`,topicId:`ph-cvs-antihypertensives`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A patient on felodipine becomes flushed and hypotensive after drinking grapefruit juice daily. The mechanism is that grapefruit juice:`,options:[`Induces intestinal P-glycoprotein`,`Chelates the drug in the gut lumen`,`Inhibits intestinal CYP3A4, raising bioavailability`,`Raises gastric pH and increases dissolution`],answer:2,explanation:`Furanocoumarins in grapefruit juice irreversibly inactivate CYP3A4 in the gut wall, so much less felodipine is destroyed during absorption and plasma levels can several-fold exceed the expected value. Induction would have the opposite effect, lowering levels, and is what rifampicin and St John wort do to the same drug.`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-antihypertensives-16`,topicId:`ph-cvs-antihypertensives`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A patient with acute aortic dissection has a blood pressure of 200/120 mmHg. The correct initial agent is:`,options:[`Sublingual nifedipine`,`Intravenous hydralazine`,`Sodium nitroprusside given alone`,`Intravenous labetalol`],answer:3,explanation:`In dissection the aim is to lower both blood pressure and the rate of rise of aortic pressure, so a beta blocking agent such as labetalol or esmolol must come first to reduce heart rate and shear stress. A pure vasodilator given alone, whether hydralazine, nifedipine or nitroprusside, causes reflex tachycardia that raises shear stress and can extend the tear, which is why nitroprusside is added only after beta blockade.`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics-9`,topicId:`ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which drug is the class IB prototype, shortening the action potential duration and acting selectively on ischaemic ventricular tissue?`,options:[`Quinidine`,`Lignocaine`,`Flecainide`,`Sotalol`],answer:1,explanation:`Lignocaine binds inactivated sodium channels with rapid on-off kinetics, so it suppresses depolarised ischaemic ventricular myocardium while barely affecting normal tissue, and it shortens the action potential. Quinidine is class IA and prolongs the action potential and the QT interval, while flecainide is class IC with marked conduction slowing and no change in duration.`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics-10`,topicId:`ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The characteristic visual disturbance of digoxin toxicity is:`,options:[`Blue-tinted vision`,`Loss of red-green colour discrimination`,`Yellow-green haloes around objects`,`Night blindness`],answer:2,explanation:`Xanthopsia, a yellow-green tinge with haloes, is the classic ocular sign of digoxin toxicity and appears along with anorexia, nausea and confusion. Blue-tinted vision belongs to sildenafil through PDE6 inhibition, and loss of red-green discrimination is the ethambutol optic neuropathy.`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics-11`,topicId:`ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Flecainide increased mortality when used to suppress ventricular ectopics after myocardial infarction. Its Vaughan Williams subclass is:`,options:[`Class IC`,`Class IA`,`Class IB`,`Class II`],answer:0,explanation:`Flecainide and propafenone are class IC agents: they dissociate very slowly from the sodium channel, so conduction slows markedly and the QRS widens, which is proarrhythmic in scarred myocardium — the finding of the CAST trial. Class IB drugs such as lignocaine dissociate rapidly and are safe in that setting, which is exactly the distinction the trial exposed.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics-12`,topicId:`ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which co-prescribed drug raises the plasma digoxin level by inhibiting P-glycoprotein?`,options:[`Rifampicin`,`Phenytoin`,`Cholestyramine`,`Quinidine`],answer:3,explanation:`Quinidine, and likewise amiodarone and verapamil, inhibit the P-glycoprotein efflux pump, reducing intestinal secretion and renal tubular secretion of digoxin so its level can roughly double. Rifampicin does the opposite by inducing P-glycoprotein, and cholestyramine simply binds digoxin in the gut and lowers absorption.`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics-13`,topicId:`ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Digoxin has a volume of distribution of 500 L and oral bioavailability of 0.7. The oral loading dose needed for a target plasma concentration of 1.5 ng/mL is about:`,options:[`750 micrograms`,`1071 micrograms`,`525 micrograms`,`1500 micrograms`],answer:1,explanation:`Loading dose = target concentration x Vd / F. A concentration of 1.5 ng/mL is 1.5 mcg/L, so 1.5 x 500 = 750 mcg would be the intravenous dose, and dividing by the bioavailability of 0.7 gives about 1071 mcg orally. The figure 750 micrograms is the trap from forgetting to correct for bioavailability, and 525 micrograms comes from multiplying by 0.7 instead of dividing.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics-14`,topicId:`ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In the combination sacubitril with valsartan used for heart failure, the sacubitril component acts by:`,options:[`Inhibiting neprilysin, so natriuretic peptides are not degraded`,`Inhibiting renin directly`,`Blocking the mineralocorticoid receptor`,`Inhibiting sodium-glucose cotransporter 2`],answer:0,explanation:`Neprilysin degrades atrial and B-type natriuretic peptides, so inhibiting it preserves their vasodilator and natriuretic actions, while valsartan blocks the angiotensin II that would otherwise be preserved as well. The combination must never be given with an ACE inhibitor and needs a 36-hour washout, because dual bradykinin sparing markedly raises the risk of angioedema.`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics-15`,topicId:`ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Before adjusting digoxin in a 70-year-old man weighing 70 kg with a serum creatinine of 1.4 mg/dL, the Cockcroft-Gault creatinine clearance is about:`,options:[`34.7 mL/min`,`41.3 mL/min`,`48.6 mL/min`,`68.1 mL/min`],answer:2,explanation:`CrCl = (140 - age) x weight / (72 x serum creatinine) = (140 - 70) x 70 / (72 x 1.4) = 4900 / 100.8, which is about 48.6 mL/min, so the digoxin maintenance dose must be cut because roughly two thirds of the drug is excreted unchanged by the kidney. The value 41.3 mL/min is the trap from applying the 0.85 correction factor, which is used only for women.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics-16`,topicId:`ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A patient with acute digoxin poisoning has a serum potassium of 6.4 mmol/L, bradycardia and atrial tachycardia with block. Which measure is CONTRAINDICATED?`,options:[`Digoxin-specific antibody Fab fragments`,`Correction of coexisting hypomagnesaemia`,`Temporary cardiac pacing for symptomatic bradycardia`,`Intravenous calcium gluconate for the hyperkalaemia`],answer:3,explanation:`Calcium is the reflex treatment for hyperkalaemia, but in digoxin toxicity intracellular calcium is already grossly raised and adding more risks intractable systolic contracture, so Fab fragments are used instead and they correct the potassium as they bind the drug. Magnesium replacement and pacing are both legitimate supportive measures.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation-9`,topicId:`ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which class of hypolipidaemic drug produces the greatest fall in plasma triglycerides?`,options:[`Ezetimibe`,`Bile acid sequestrants`,`Fibrates`,`Statins`],answer:2,explanation:`Fibrates activate PPAR-alpha, which induces lipoprotein lipase and suppresses apo C-III, lowering triglycerides by 30-50 percent, so they are chosen for severe hypertriglyceridaemia and to prevent pancreatitis. Bile acid sequestrants are the worst option here because they lower LDL but can actually raise triglycerides.`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation-10`,topicId:`ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which drug dissolves an already formed thrombus by converting plasminogen to plasmin and is relatively fibrin-selective?`,options:[`Alteplase`,`Streptokinase`,`Heparin`,`Aspirin`],answer:0,explanation:`Alteplase is recombinant tissue plasminogen activator, active mainly on plasminogen already bound to fibrin, so lysis is concentrated at the clot and it is neither antigenic nor allergenic. Streptokinase also activates plasminogen but does so throughout the circulation, causing a systemic lytic state, and being a bacterial protein it is antigenic and cannot be repeated.`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation-11`,topicId:`ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A patient on simvastatin is given gemfibrozil and develops severe muscle pain, dark urine and a creatine kinase of 8000 U/L. The best explanation is:`,options:[`Statin-induced autoimmune hepatitis`,`Gemfibrozil raises statin exposure, sharply increasing the risk of rhabdomyolysis`,`Gemfibrozil antagonises the statin at HMG-CoA reductase`,`Both drugs block intestinal sterol absorption`],answer:1,explanation:`Gemfibrozil inhibits statin glucuronidation and the OATP1B1 hepatic uptake transporter, so statin concentrations rise and myopathy can progress to rhabdomyolysis with myoglobinuric renal failure. Fenofibrate is the safer partner for a statin because it does not interfere with these pathways, which is why the combination question hinges on which fibrate is chosen.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation-12`,topicId:`ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which of the following is an orally active DIRECT inhibitor of factor Xa?`,options:[`Dabigatran`,`Fondaparinux`,`Warfarin`,`Rivaroxaban`],answer:3,explanation:`Rivaroxaban, like apixaban and edoxaban, binds the active site of factor Xa itself and needs no cofactor, which is why no routine monitoring is required. Fondaparinux is the near-miss: it also targets factor Xa but only indirectly, by binding antithrombin, and it must be injected subcutaneously.`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation-13`,topicId:`ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Enoxaparin differs from unfractionated heparin chiefly in that it:`,options:[`Inhibits factor Xa far more than thrombin and has predictable kinetics needing no aPTT monitoring`,`Has a much shorter half-life`,`Is completely neutralised by protamine sulphate`,`Can be given by mouth`],answer:0,explanation:`The short chains of a low molecular weight heparin can bridge antithrombin to factor Xa but are too short to bridge it to thrombin, so anti-Xa activity dominates and the dose-response is predictable enough for weight-based dosing without aPTT monitoring. Protamine is the trap: it neutralises unfractionated heparin completely but only about 60 percent of the anti-Xa activity of a low molecular weight heparin.`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation-14`,topicId:`ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`When warfarin is started for deep vein thrombosis, heparin is continued for at least five days because:`,options:[`Heparin is needed to absorb warfarin from the gut`,`Warfarin has no effect until the INR exceeds 4`,`Circulating clotting factors must decay first, and early loss of protein C makes the patient transiently hypercoagulable`,`Heparin prevents the hepatotoxicity of warfarin`],answer:2,explanation:`Warfarin stops synthesis but does not touch factors already in the blood, and prothrombin has a half-life near 60 hours, so full effect takes days; meanwhile protein C, with a half-life of only 8 hours, falls first and creates a prothrombotic window that can cause skin necrosis. Heparin covers that window, which is why the overlap continues until the INR has been in range for two consecutive days.`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation-15`,topicId:`ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A patient on warfarin has an INR of 8 with no bleeding. The most appropriate management is:`,options:[`Continue the same dose and recheck in a week`,`Withhold warfarin and give a small oral dose of vitamin K, then recheck the INR`,`Give 10 mg of vitamin K intravenously`,`Transfuse fresh frozen plasma immediately`],answer:1,explanation:`With a high INR but no bleeding, stopping warfarin and giving low-dose oral vitamin K (typically 1-2.5 mg) brings the INR down over 24 hours without over-correcting. A large intravenous dose is the trap because it causes prolonged warfarin resistance for a week or more, and fresh frozen plasma or prothrombin complex concentrate is reserved for active major bleeding.`},{id:`q-ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation-16`,topicId:`ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A patient on clopidogrel after coronary stenting is prescribed omeprazole and later suffers stent thrombosis. The interaction arises because omeprazole:`,options:[`Displaces clopidogrel from plasma albumin`,`Directly increases platelet aggregation`,`Induces CYP3A4 and clears clopidogrel faster`,`Inhibits CYP2C19, which is required to activate the clopidogrel prodrug`],answer:3,explanation:`Clopidogrel is an inactive thienopyridine that must be oxidised by CYP2C19 to its thiol metabolite before it can block the P2Y12 receptor, so a CYP2C19 inhibitor leaves the patient effectively unprotected — the same happens in genetic poor metabolisers. Pantoprazole is the preferred acid suppressant because it inhibits CYP2C19 far less, and prasugrel or ticagrelor avoid the problem altogether.`,tag:`Frequently asked`}],n=[{id:`f-ph-general-principles-1`,topicId:`ph-general-principles`,front:`Formula for therapeutic index?`,back:`TI = LD50 / ED50. Higher value means a safer drug.`},{id:`f-ph-general-principles-2`,topicId:`ph-general-principles`,front:`Competitive antagonist: what happens to the dose-response curve?`,back:`Parallel shift to the right; Emax unchanged; surmountable by more agonist.`},{id:`f-ph-general-principles-3`,topicId:`ph-general-principles`,front:`Potency versus efficacy — which axis?`,back:`Potency is on the dose (x) axis as ED50; efficacy is on the effect (y) axis as Emax.`},{id:`f-ph-general-principles-4`,topicId:`ph-general-principles`,front:`Name the four receptor superfamilies with one drug each.`,back:`Ion channel (diazepam), GPCR (propranolol), enzyme-linked (insulin), nuclear (dexamethasone).`},{id:`f-ph-general-principles-5`,topicId:`ph-general-principles`,front:`ADR types A to F?`,back:`Augmented, Bizarre, Chronic, Delayed, End-of-use, Failure of therapy.`},{id:`f-ph-general-principles-6`,topicId:`ph-general-principles`,front:`Tolerance versus tachyphylaxis?`,back:`Tolerance takes days to weeks (receptor down-regulation); tachyphylaxis takes minutes to hours (store depletion, e.g. ephedrine).`},{id:`f-ph-ans-cholinergic-1`,topicId:`ph-ans-cholinergic`,front:`Which muscarinic receptors are Gq and which are Gi?`,back:`Odd (M1, M3, M5) are Gq; even (M2, M4) are Gi.`},{id:`f-ph-ans-cholinergic-2`,topicId:`ph-ans-cholinergic`,front:`Antidote combination for organophosphate poisoning?`,back:`Atropine for muscarinic effects plus pralidoxime (2-PAM) for nicotinic effects, given before enzyme ageing.`},{id:`f-ph-ans-cholinergic-3`,topicId:`ph-ans-cholinergic`,front:`Neostigmine or physostigmine for atropine poisoning?`,back:`Physostigmine — it is a tertiary amine and crosses the blood-brain barrier.`},{id:`f-ph-ans-cholinergic-4`,topicId:`ph-ans-cholinergic`,front:`Two signs that identify a non-depolarising block?`,back:`Fade on tetanic stimulation and post-tetanic potentiation; reversed by neostigmine.`},{id:`f-ph-ans-cholinergic-5`,topicId:`ph-ans-cholinergic`,front:`Neuromuscular blocker of choice in renal and hepatic failure?`,back:`Atracurium — eliminated by spontaneous Hofmann degradation.`},{id:`f-ph-ans-cholinergic-6`,topicId:`ph-ans-cholinergic`,front:`Edrophonium test result in cholinergic crisis?`,back:`Weakness worsens (it improves in myasthenic crisis).`},{id:`f-ph-ans-adrenergic-1`,topicId:`ph-ans-adrenergic`,front:`Rate-limiting enzyme of catecholamine synthesis?`,back:`Tyrosine hydroxylase (tyrosine to DOPA).`},{id:`f-ph-ans-adrenergic-2`,topicId:`ph-ans-adrenergic`,front:`Dopamine effects by dose?`,back:`Low = D1 renal vasodilation; medium = beta-1 inotropy; high = alpha-1 vasoconstriction.`},{id:`f-ph-ans-adrenergic-3`,topicId:`ph-ans-adrenergic`,front:`Phenoxybenzamine versus phentolamine?`,back:`Phenoxybenzamine is irreversible and long acting; phentolamine is competitive and short acting.`},{id:`f-ph-ans-adrenergic-4`,topicId:`ph-ans-adrenergic`,front:`Beta blockers with intrinsic sympathomimetic activity?`,back:`Pindolol, acebutolol, celiprolol.`},{id:`f-ph-ans-adrenergic-5`,topicId:`ph-ans-adrenergic`,front:`Order of blockade in phaeochromocytoma?`,back:`Alpha blocker first, then beta blocker — never the reverse.`},{id:`f-ph-ans-adrenergic-6`,topicId:`ph-ans-adrenergic`,front:`Final urinary metabolite of noradrenaline?`,back:`VMA (vanillylmandelic acid), via MAO and COMT.`},{id:`f-ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics-1`,topicId:`ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics`,front:`Benzodiazepine versus barbiturate at the chloride channel?`,back:`Benzodiazepines increase frequency of opening; barbiturates increase duration.`},{id:`f-ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics-2`,topicId:`ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics`,front:`Antidote for benzodiazepine overdose?`,back:`Flumazenil IV; resedation is likely because its half-life is shorter.`},{id:`f-ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics-3`,topicId:`ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics`,front:`Drug of choice for absence seizures and its mechanism?`,back:`Ethosuximide — blocks thalamic T-type calcium channels.`},{id:`f-ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics-4`,topicId:`ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics`,front:`Why does phenytoin show a disproportionate rise in level?`,back:`Its metabolising enzymes saturate within the therapeutic range, so kinetics become zero order.`},{id:`f-ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics-5`,topicId:`ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics`,front:`Target of levetiracetam?`,back:`Synaptic vesicle protein SV2A.`},{id:`f-ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics-6`,topicId:`ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics`,front:`First drug in status epilepticus?`,back:`IV lorazepam, then IV fosphenytoin, valproate or levetiracetam.`},{id:`f-ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants-1`,topicId:`ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants`,front:`Which dopamine pathway gives the antipsychotic benefit?`,back:`Mesolimbic. Nigrostriatal gives EPS, tuberoinfundibular gives hyperprolactinaemia.`},{id:`f-ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants-2`,topicId:`ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants`,front:`Order of extrapyramidal reactions over time?`,back:`Acute dystonia, akathisia, parkinsonism, tardive dyskinesia.`},{id:`f-ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants-3`,topicId:`ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants`,front:`Treatment of neuroleptic malignant syndrome?`,back:`Stop the drug, cool, give dantrolene plus bromocriptine or amantadine.`},{id:`f-ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants-4`,topicId:`ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants`,front:`Antidote-style treatment for serotonin syndrome?`,back:`Cyproheptadine, a 5-HT2 antagonist, plus supportive cooling.`},{id:`f-ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants-5`,topicId:`ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants`,front:`Therapeutic plasma range for lithium?`,back:`0.6-1.2 mEq/L; toxicity above 1.5 mEq/L.`},{id:`f-ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants-6`,topicId:`ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants`,front:`Mandatory monitoring with clozapine and why?`,back:`White cell and neutrophil counts — risk of agranulocytosis.`},{id:`f-ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics-1`,topicId:`ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics`,front:`Opioid overdose triad?`,back:`Pinpoint pupils, respiratory depression and coma. Reverse with naloxone.`},{id:`f-ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics-2`,topicId:`ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics`,front:`Two morphine effects to which tolerance never develops?`,back:`Miosis and constipation.`},{id:`f-ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics-3`,topicId:`ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics`,front:`What do MAC and blood-gas coefficient each measure?`,back:`MAC measures potency (low MAC = potent); blood-gas coefficient measures speed (low value = fast induction).`},{id:`f-ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics-4`,topicId:`ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics`,front:`Treatment of malignant hyperthermia?`,back:`Dantrolene 2.5 mg/kg IV, stop the trigger, active cooling.`},{id:`f-ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics-5`,topicId:`ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics`,front:`Why do local anaesthetics fail in inflamed tissue?`,back:`Acidic pH ionises the weak base, so less unionised drug crosses the nerve membrane.`},{id:`f-ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics-6`,topicId:`ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics`,front:`Why add carbidopa to levodopa?`,back:`It inhibits peripheral DOPA decarboxylase only, cutting the dose by about 75 percent and reducing nausea.`},{id:`f-ph-cvs-antihypertensives-1`,topicId:`ph-cvs-antihypertensives`,front:`Why do ACE inhibitors cause cough but ARBs do not?`,back:`ACE (kininase II) inhibition lets bradykinin and substance P accumulate; ARBs do not affect bradykinin.`},{id:`f-ph-cvs-antihypertensives-2`,topicId:`ph-cvs-antihypertensives`,front:`Antihypertensives of choice in pregnancy?`,back:`Labetalol, methyldopa, nifedipine. ACE inhibitors and ARBs are contraindicated.`},{id:`f-ph-cvs-antihypertensives-3`,topicId:`ph-cvs-antihypertensives`,front:`Main antianginal action of nitrates?`,back:`Venodilation lowers preload and therefore myocardial oxygen demand.`},{id:`f-ph-cvs-antihypertensives-4`,topicId:`ph-cvs-antihypertensives`,front:`How is nitrate tolerance prevented?`,back:`A daily nitrate-free interval of 8-12 hours, usually overnight.`},{id:`f-ph-cvs-antihypertensives-5`,topicId:`ph-cvs-antihypertensives`,front:`Why avoid beta blockers in variant angina?`,back:`Unopposed alpha-1 vasoconstriction after loss of beta-2 dilation worsens coronary spasm.`},{id:`f-ph-cvs-antihypertensives-6`,topicId:`ph-cvs-antihypertensives`,front:`Toxicity of prolonged sodium nitroprusside infusion?`,back:`Cyanide and thiocyanate accumulation; treat with sodium thiosulphate or hydroxocobalamin.`},{id:`f-ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics-1`,topicId:`ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics`,front:`Mechanism of digoxin inotropy?`,back:`Na-K ATPase inhibition raises intracellular sodium, then calcium via the Na-Ca exchanger.`},{id:`f-ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics-2`,topicId:`ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics`,front:`Commonest precipitant of digoxin toxicity?`,back:`Hypokalaemia, usually from a thiazide or loop diuretic.`},{id:`f-ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics-3`,topicId:`ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics`,front:`Vaughan Williams classes I to IV?`,back:`I sodium block, II beta block, III potassium block, IV calcium block.`},{id:`f-ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics-4`,topicId:`ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics`,front:`Which heart failure drugs improve survival?`,back:`ACE inhibitor or ARNI, evidence-based beta blocker, mineralocorticoid antagonist and SGLT2 inhibitor.`},{id:`f-ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics-5`,topicId:`ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics`,front:`Drug of choice for paroxysmal SVT?`,back:`Adenosine IV bolus, half-life under 10 seconds.`},{id:`f-ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics-6`,topicId:`ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics`,front:`Main organ toxicities of amiodarone?`,back:`Thyroid (both directions), lung fibrosis, corneal deposits, skin discolouration, liver, neuropathy.`},{id:`f-ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation-1`,topicId:`ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation`,front:`Statin mechanism in one line?`,back:`HMG-CoA reductase inhibition, which up-regulates hepatic LDL receptors.`},{id:`f-ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation-2`,topicId:`ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation`,front:`Antidotes: heparin, warfarin, dabigatran?`,back:`Protamine sulphate; vitamin K with FFP or PCC; idarucizumab.`},{id:`f-ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation-3`,topicId:`ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation`,front:`Monitoring test for heparin and for warfarin?`,back:`aPTT for heparin (1.5-2.5 x control); INR for warfarin (target 2-3).`},{id:`f-ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation-4`,topicId:`ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation`,front:`Which anticoagulant is safe in pregnancy and why?`,back:`Heparin — a large polar molecule that does not cross the placenta.`},{id:`f-ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation-5`,topicId:`ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation`,front:`Why is the antiplatelet dose of aspirin only 75-150 mg?`,back:`Higher doses also inhibit endothelial COX-2 and reduce protective prostacyclin.`},{id:`f-ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation-6`,topicId:`ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation`,front:`Type II heparin-induced thrombocytopenia — key paradox and treatment?`,back:`Low platelets but thrombosis; stop all heparin and give a direct thrombin inhibitor such as argatroban.`},{id:`f-ph-general-principles-7`,topicId:`ph-general-principles`,front:`Certain safety factor formula?`,back:`CSF = LD1 / ED99 — stricter than the therapeutic index because it compares the overlapping tails.`},{id:`f-ph-general-principles-8`,topicId:`ph-general-principles`,front:`What does pA2 measure?`,back:`Negative log of the molar antagonist concentration giving a dose ratio of 2; a higher pA2 means a more potent competitive antagonist.`},{id:`f-ph-general-principles-9`,topicId:`ph-general-principles`,front:`Name three prodrugs and their active forms.`,back:`Enalapril to enalaprilat, levodopa to dopamine, codeine to morphine via CYP2D6.`},{id:`f-ph-general-principles-10`,topicId:`ph-general-principles`,front:`Chemical antagonism — give two examples.`,back:`Chelation: dimercaprol with arsenic; calcium with tetracycline forming an unabsorbable complex.`},{id:`f-ph-ans-cholinergic-7`,topicId:`ph-ans-cholinergic`,front:`Order in which atropine effects appear as dose rises?`,back:`Dry mouth and less sweating, then tachycardia, then mydriasis and cycloplegia, then urinary retention, then delirium.`},{id:`f-ph-ans-cholinergic-8`,topicId:`ph-ans-cholinergic`,front:`Why is succinylcholine dangerous in burns and denervation?`,back:`Up-regulated extrajunctional nicotinic receptors release a huge potassium load — hyperkalaemic cardiac arrest.`},{id:`f-ph-ans-cholinergic-9`,topicId:`ph-ans-cholinergic`,front:`How does sugammadex work?`,back:`A gamma-cyclodextrin that encapsulates rocuronium or vecuronium in plasma; no anticholinesterase, so no muscarinic effects.`},{id:`f-ph-ans-cholinergic-10`,topicId:`ph-ans-cholinergic`,front:`Cholinergic crisis versus myasthenic crisis — what to do?`,back:`Cholinergic: stop the anticholinesterase, give atropine, ventilate. Myasthenic: increase the anticholinesterase and treat the precipitant.`},{id:`f-ph-ans-adrenergic-7`,topicId:`ph-ans-adrenergic`,front:`Mechanism of reserpine?`,back:`Irreversible VMAT-2 blockade — amines cannot be stored, so cytoplasmic MAO destroys them.`},{id:`f-ph-ans-adrenergic-8`,topicId:`ph-ans-adrenergic`,front:`Treatment of noradrenaline extravasation?`,back:`Local infiltration of phentolamine to reverse alpha-1 vasoconstriction.`},{id:`f-ph-ans-adrenergic-9`,topicId:`ph-ans-adrenergic`,front:`Which beta blockers vasodilate, and how?`,back:`Carvedilol and labetalol by alpha-1 blockade; nebivolol by nitric oxide release.`},{id:`f-ph-ans-adrenergic-10`,topicId:`ph-ans-adrenergic`,front:`Metabolic effects of high-dose beta-2 agonists?`,back:`Hypokalaemia (potassium shifts into cells), tremor, hyperglycaemia and lactic acidosis.`},{id:`f-ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics-7`,topicId:`ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics`,front:`Michaelis-Menten dosing equation for phenytoin?`,back:`Dose rate = Vmax x Css / (Km + Css). Km is about 4 mcg/mL in adults.`},{id:`f-ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics-8`,topicId:`ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics`,front:`Drug of choice for trigeminal neuralgia?`,back:`Carbamazepine (oxcarbazepine as the alternative).`},{id:`f-ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics-9`,topicId:`ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics`,front:`Which antiepileptic is most teratogenic?`,back:`Sodium valproate — neural tube defects and lowered childhood IQ; avoid in women of childbearing potential.`},{id:`f-ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics-10`,topicId:`ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics`,front:`GABA-enhancing mechanisms: vigabatrin, tiagabine, gabapentin?`,back:`Vigabatrin inhibits GABA transaminase; tiagabine blocks GAT-1 reuptake; gabapentin binds the alpha-2-delta calcium channel subunit.`},{id:`f-ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants-7`,topicId:`ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants`,front:`NMS versus serotonin syndrome — the discriminating signs?`,back:`NMS: lead-pipe rigidity, hyporeflexia, normal pupils, onset over days. Serotonin syndrome: clonus, hyperreflexia, mydriasis, diarrhoea, onset in hours.`},{id:`f-ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants-8`,topicId:`ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants`,front:`Specific treatment of tricyclic antidepressant overdose?`,back:`Intravenous sodium bicarbonate for the widened QRS, hypotension and arrhythmia.`},{id:`f-ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants-9`,topicId:`ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants`,front:`Which antidepressant most lowers the seizure threshold?`,back:`Bupropion — avoid in epilepsy, bulimia and anorexia nervosa.`},{id:`f-ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants-10`,topicId:`ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants`,front:`Treatment of lithium-induced nephrogenic diabetes insipidus?`,back:`Amiloride — it blocks the epithelial sodium channel through which lithium enters; thiazides would push lithium levels up.`},{id:`f-ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics-7`,topicId:`ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics`,front:`Which opioid must never be given with an MAO inhibitor?`,back:`Pethidine — it blocks serotonin reuptake and causes hyperthermia, rigidity and agitation.`},{id:`f-ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics-8`,topicId:`ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics`,front:`Approximate MAC values worth remembering?`,back:`Nitrous oxide 105 percent, sevoflurane 2, isoflurane 1.15, halothane 0.75. Low MAC means high potency.`},{id:`f-ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics-9`,topicId:`ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics`,front:`Ester versus amide local anaesthetics?`,back:`Amides have two i sounds in the name (lignocaine, bupivacaine) and are metabolised by liver CYP; esters are hydrolysed by plasma cholinesterase to PABA and cause more allergy.`},{id:`f-ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics-10`,topicId:`ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics`,front:`Why avoid etomidate infusion in the critically ill?`,back:`It inhibits 11-beta-hydroxylase and suppresses cortisol synthesis for up to 24 hours.`},{id:`f-ph-cvs-antihypertensives-7`,topicId:`ph-cvs-antihypertensives`,front:`First-line antihypertensive in a diabetic with albuminuria?`,back:`ACE inhibitor or ARB — efferent arteriolar dilation lowers intraglomerular pressure and proteinuria.`},{id:`f-ph-cvs-antihypertensives-8`,topicId:`ph-cvs-antihypertensives`,front:`Which antihypertensive causes drug-induced lupus in slow acetylators?`,back:`Hydralazine (also procainamide and isoniazid). Methyldopa instead gives a positive Coombs test.`},{id:`f-ph-cvs-antihypertensives-9`,topicId:`ph-cvs-antihypertensives`,front:`Why is an ACE inhibitor never combined with an ARB?`,back:`Dual RAAS blockade adds hyperkalaemia, hypotension and acute kidney injury with no outcome benefit.`},{id:`f-ph-cvs-antihypertensives-10`,topicId:`ph-cvs-antihypertensives`,front:`Diuretic sites: thiazide, loop, potassium-sparing?`,back:`Thiazide at the DCT Na-Cl symporter; loop at NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb; amiloride and spironolactone at the collecting duct.`},{id:`f-ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics-7`,topicId:`ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics`,front:`Class I subclasses with one example each?`,back:`IA quinidine (prolongs APD), IB lignocaine (shortens APD, ventricular only), IC flecainide (marked conduction slowing, proarrhythmic post-MI).`},{id:`f-ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics-8`,topicId:`ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics`,front:`Loading dose formula, and digoxin Vd?`,back:`LD = target concentration x Vd / F. Digoxin Vd is about 7 L/kg, roughly 500 L in an adult.`},{id:`f-ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics-9`,topicId:`ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics`,front:`Which drugs raise digoxin levels?`,back:`Quinidine, amiodarone and verapamil by inhibiting P-glycoprotein; diuretics indirectly through hypokalaemia.`},{id:`f-ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics-10`,topicId:`ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics`,front:`Cockcroft-Gault equation?`,back:`CrCl (mL/min) = (140 - age) x weight in kg / (72 x serum creatinine in mg/dL); multiply by 0.85 for women.`},{id:`f-ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation-7`,topicId:`ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation`,front:`Which lipid drug lowers triglycerides most, and how?`,back:`Fibrates — PPAR-alpha activation induces lipoprotein lipase and suppresses apo C-III.`},{id:`f-ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation-8`,topicId:`ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation`,front:`Why is a statin plus gemfibrozil risky?`,back:`Gemfibrozil blocks statin glucuronidation and OATP1B1 uptake, so statin levels rise and rhabdomyolysis follows; fenofibrate is safer.`},{id:`f-ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation-9`,topicId:`ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation`,front:`Targets of dabigatran, rivaroxaban and fondaparinux?`,back:`Dabigatran: thrombin directly. Rivaroxaban and apixaban: factor Xa directly. Fondaparinux: factor Xa indirectly through antithrombin.`},{id:`f-ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation-10`,topicId:`ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation`,front:`Why does omeprazole weaken clopidogrel?`,back:`Clopidogrel is a prodrug activated by CYP2C19, which omeprazole inhibits; use pantoprazole instead.`}];export{n as flashcards,e as notes,t as questions};